(function(){
'use strict';
const D=globalThis.RA.D;
const q=(prompt,answer,d1,d2,explain,sense=null,type='meaning')=>({prompt,answer,d1,d2,explain,sense,type});
const C=(senseId,domain,en,zh,structure,grammar,rationale)=>({senseId,domain,en,zh,structure,grammar,rationale,sourceType:'authored'});
const M=(wrong,right,reason,errorType='meaning')=>({errorType,wrong,right,reason});
const E=(origin,w)=>({origin,prefix:null,root:null,suffix:null,source:'Etymonline: https://www.etymonline.com/word/'+w.toLowerCase().replaceAll(' ','-')});
const sensePlan={subject:[1,2,4,3],subjective:[1,1,2,1],subjugate:[1,2,1,1],submarine:[1,2,1,1],submerge:[1,2,1,1],submission:[1,2,3,1],submit:[1,2,1,1],subordinate:[1,2,3,1],subscribe:[1,2,3,1],subsequent:[1,1,2,1],subside:[1,2,1,2],subsidize:[1,2,1,2],subsidy:[1,2,1,1],subsistence:[1,2,1,2],substance:[1,2,3,1],substantial:[1,2,2,1],substantially:[1,2,1,2],substantive:[1,2,1,2],substitute:[1,2,2,1],subtitle:[1,2,1,2],subtle:[1,2,1,2],subtlety:[1,2,1,2],subtract:[1,1,2,2],suburb:[1,2,1,2],subway:[1,2,1,2],succeed:[1,2,1,2],success:[1,2,1,1],successful:[1,2,1,1],succession:[1,2,1,2],succumb:[1,2,1,1]};
const P=(w,origin,structure,en,zh,domain,grammar,rationale,m,qs)=>D(w,{etymology:E(origin,w),contexts:[C(w+'.s1',domain,en,zh,structure,grammar,rationale)],mistakes:[m],quiz:qs.map((x,i)=>({id:w+'.q'+(i+1),senseId:w+'.s'+(x.sense??sensePlan[w]?.[i]??1),type:'辨义与用法',prompt:x.prompt,options:[x.answer,x.d1,x.d2],answer:x.answer,explain:x.explain,errorType:x.type}))});
P('subfreezing','Formed from sub- “below” and freezing, the -ing form of freeze. The prefix marks a temperature below the freezing point.','sub- below + freezing','A subfreezing wind crossed the open field before sunrise.','清晨日出前，一阵低于冰点的风吹过空旷的田野。','weather','Adjective before a noun: subfreezing + noun.','The adjective describes temperature below 0°C (32°F), rather than merely cold weather.',M('The water stayed liquid at a subfreezing temperature of 8°C.','The water stayed liquid at a temperature of 8°C, above freezing.','Eight degrees Celsius is above the freezing point; “subfreezing” means below 0°C.','measurement'),[
q('In the forecast, what does “subfreezing overnight lows” tell hikers?','Temperatures will fall below 0°C.','Temperatures will remain above 0°C.','Temperatures will stay exactly at 0°C.','“Subfreezing” places the temperature below the freezing point.'),
q('Choose the sentence in which “subfreezing” has its temperature sense.','The subfreezing air froze the puddles.','The subfreezing debate had no clear winner.','She subfreezing the soup before lunch.','Here it modifies “air” and means below freezing; it is not a verb or a synonym for disputed.'),
q('Which measurement is necessarily subfreezing on the Celsius scale?','−4°C','4°C','40°C','A negative Celsius temperature is below 0°C.'),
q('Why is “subfreezing” more precise than simply “cold” in a weather warning?','It states that the temperature is below the freezing point.','It states that the wind is unusually fast.','It states that the air is very dry.','The prefix sub- means below, so the word specifies a temperature threshold.')
]);
P('subglacial','Built from sub- “under” and glacial, from Latin glacies “ice.”','sub- under + glacial','Scientists mapped a subglacial lake sealed beneath several kilometers of Antarctic ice.','科学家绘制了一座被数公里厚南极冰层封闭的冰川底部湖泊。','glaciology','Adjective: subglacial + landform or process.','The lake lies beneath glacier ice; “subglacial” does not mean merely near a glacier.',M('The subglacial valley lies on top of the glacier.','The subglacial valley lies beneath the glacier.','“Subglacial” locates something under glacial ice, not on its upper surface.'),[
q('Where would researchers expect to find a subglacial lake?','Beneath a glacier’s ice.','On a glacier’s exposed summit.','In a warm ocean far from ice.','The prefix sub- means under, and glacial refers to glacier ice.'),
q('Which observation supports calling a channel subglacial?','It runs under an ice sheet.','It runs beside a snow-free beach.','It is carved into a dry desert dune.','A subglacial feature is located below glacial ice.'),
q('In “subglacial meltwater,” what does the adjective contribute?','The water is beneath glacier ice.','The water is chemically pure.','The water has frozen solid.','The word locates the meltwater; it does not describe purity or a frozen state.'),
q('Which contrast is accurate?','A subglacial stream flows beneath glacier ice; a surface stream flows on it.','A subglacial stream flows above glacier ice; a surface stream flows below it.','Both terms mean a stream in open seawater.','“Subglacial” means under glacial ice, while “surface” marks the top.')
]);
P('subject','From Latin subiectus, past participle of subicere “to place under”; the senses developed from something placed before or under consideration.','Latin subiectus, from subicere “place under”','The documentary’s subject is how coastal towns adapt to rising seas.','这部纪录片的主题是沿海城镇如何适应海平面上升。','communication','Noun sense 1: the subject of a book, study, or discussion.','This sentence uses the topic sense, not a school discipline, grammatical subject, or verb meaning to expose someone to something.',M('The documentary subject about coastal flooding were clear.','The documentary’s subject was coastal flooding.','“Subject” as a topic is singular here, so it takes “was”; the original also lacks a complete construction.','grammar'),[
q('A report titled “The Subject of Sleep” mainly tells you what?','Sleep is the topic being discussed.','Sleep is the report’s grammatical verb.','The report is a person ruled by a king.','For a text, the subject is its topic.'),
q('Which use of “subject” means an area studied at school?','Chemistry is her strongest subject.','The subject of the film is migration.','The king addressed his subjects.','A school course is one of the senses listed for subject.'),
q('In “The experiment subjected the sample to heat,” what did the experiment do?','Forced the sample to undergo heat.','Named the sample as the main topic.','Made the sample a noun in a sentence.','The verb subject means force someone or something to experience a condition.',4),
q('What does “The patient was subject to sudden dizziness” mean?','The patient was liable to experience sudden dizziness.','The patient was the topic of a lecture.','The patient forced someone else to feel dizzy.','The adjective construction “subject to” means liable or exposed to something.',undefined,'collocation')
]);
P('subject matter','A compound of subject, ultimately from Latin subiectus, and matter, from Latin materia “material”; together they mean the topic or content under discussion.','subject + matter; matter from Latin materia “material”','The museum label explains the subject matter of the painting: a harvest festival.','博物館標籤說明了這幅畫的題材：一場豐收節慶。','art','Noun phrase: subject matter of + work or discussion.','It names what a work deals with, not the physical material from which it is made.',M('The canvas subject matter is oil and linen.','The canvas is made of linen and the subject matter is a harvest festival.','Oil and linen describe the painting’s materials; its subject matter is what it depicts.','word-choice'),[
q('A review says a novel’s subject matter is exile. What is being identified?','The topic the novel deals with.','The paper used to print the novel.','The author’s home address.','Subject matter is the content or topic of a work.'),
q('Which sentence uses “subject matter” naturally?','The lecture’s subject matter was early astronomy.','The lecture subject mattered early astronomy.','The subject matter of the telescope weighs two kilograms.','The phrase names a topic and commonly follows “of” or a possessive.'),
q('A painting shows a shipwreck but is painted in oil. Which is its subject matter?','A shipwreck.','Oil paint.','The wooden frame.','The depicted topic is the subject matter; oil is the medium.'),
q('Why might a school give an age warning based on a film’s subject matter?','Its themes or content may be unsuitable for some viewers.','Its projector may be made of heavy material.','Its running time is exactly one hour.','Subject matter refers to themes and content, which can affect suitability.')
]);
P('subjection','From Latin subjectionem, from subicere “to place under”; the noun denotes the state of being placed under another’s control.','Latin subjectionem, from subicere “place under”','The treaty ended the island’s subjection to a foreign power.','這項條約結束了該島受外國勢力統治的狀態。','history','Noun: subjection to + authority or control.','The word refers to a condition of domination, not ordinary cooperation or a single submitted document.',M('The colony celebrated its subjection to self-rule.','The colony celebrated its freedom from subjection to foreign rule.','Subjection means being under another power’s control, which conflicts with self-rule.','meaning'),[
q('If a people live in subjection, what is their political condition?','They are controlled by another power.','They govern themselves without outside control.','They have submitted a research paper.','Subjection is the state of being under another’s control.'),
q('Which preposition completes the usual phrase: “subjection ___ an empire”?','to','for','among','The established construction is “subjection to” a controlling power.'),
q('What would most clearly end a region’s subjection?','It gains independence from the ruling power.','It receives a new map from the ruling power.','It changes its flag while remaining governed.','Independence ends the condition of being controlled by another power.'),
q('How does “subjection” differ from “submission” in this sentence? “Years of subjection shaped the province.”','It emphasizes the condition of being ruled.','It means a document handed in for review.','It names a school subject.','Subjection denotes an ongoing state under control, not a submitted document.')
]);
P('subjective','Formed from subject + -ive; subject traces to Latin subiectus. The adjective contrasts personal experience or opinion with externally measurable fact.','subject + -ive; subject from Latin subiectus','Whether the melody feels joyful is subjective, so listeners may disagree.','這段旋律聽起來是否愉快是主觀感受，因此聽眾可能意見不同。','arts','Adjective: subjective judgment, impression, or experience.','It is based on a person’s feelings or perspective; the medical sense can describe symptoms reported by a patient.',M('The room’s temperature was subjective at exactly 18°C on a calibrated thermometer.','The room’s measured temperature was 18°C; whether it felt comfortable was subjective.','A thermometer reading is objective measurement, while comfort is personal experience.','distinction'),[
q('A critic calls a review subjective. What does that suggest?','It reflects personal opinion or feeling.','It reports only independently measured facts.','It gives a mathematically proven result.','Subjective judgments depend on individual perspective.'),
q('Which is the clearest subjective statement?','This song feels more moving to me than the last one.','The song lasts three minutes and twelve seconds.','The recording contains two violin tracks.','A personal response is subjective; duration and track count are measurable.'),
q('A patient reports subjective pain that cannot be measured directly. Which sense is intended?','A symptom felt by the patient.','A symptom visible in every scan.','An opinion expressed by the physician.','The clinical sense contrasts the patient’s felt experience with externally measured signs.'),
q('Why should a survey describe “the best design” as a subjective rating?','People may judge it differently according to their preferences.','The design has no colors.','The survey has already measured its exact weight.','Evaluations of what is best depend on personal taste.')
]);
P('subjugate','From Latin subiugare “to bring under the yoke,” from sub- “under” and iugum “yoke.”','Latin subiugare “bring under the yoke”','The invading army tried to subjugate the neighboring kingdom.','入侵軍隊試圖征服鄰近王國。','history','Verb: subjugate a people, territory, or group.','The verb implies forceful control; a related sense can describe cruel oppression of a group.',M('The neighboring kingdom subjugated the invaders by welcoming them as equal allies.','The neighboring kingdom allied with the invaders as equal partners.','Subjugate means to bring under control, not to form an equal alliance.','meaning'),[
q('What did the empire aim to do when it sought to subjugate a province?','Bring it under its control by force.','Invite it to govern independently.','Study its history without governing it.','Subjugate means to make a country or group completely subject to control.'),
q('Which action best fits the oppressive sense of “subjugate”?','A regime strips a minority group of freedom and forces obedience.','A council invites residents to vote on a proposal.','A teacher asks students to compare two maps.','The oppressive sense concerns cruel treatment that denies a group freedom.'),
q('Choose the grammatical form: “The ruler tried to ___ the rebels.”','subjugate','subjugation','subjugatedly','After “tried to,” the base verb is required.'),
q('Why is “subjugate” stronger than “persuade” in a report of conquest?','It implies forceful control rather than voluntary agreement.','It means to ask a polite question.','It describes a temporary change in temperature.','Subjugation is coercive domination, unlike persuasion.')
]);
P('subjugation','From Latin subjugationem, noun of action from subiugare “bring under the yoke.”','Latin subjugationem, from subiugare “bring under the yoke”','The novel traces the community’s resistance to centuries of subjugation.','這部小說描寫該社群如何反抗數百年來的奴役與支配。','history','Noun: subjugation of + people or group.','It describes a state or process of coercive domination, not voluntary cooperation.',M('The people welcomed subjugation because they remained entirely free from outside rule.','The people rejected subjugation because it placed them under outside rule.','Subjugation entails being forced under another power, so it is incompatible with complete independence.','meaning'),[
q('What does “the subjugation of a territory” refer to?','Its forced domination by another power.','Its voluntary partnership with an equal ally.','Its geological formation beneath ice.','Subjugation is the state of being forced under another power.'),
q('Which phrase correctly completes the noun construction?','the subjugation of the population','the subjugation to ruled the population','the subjugation for free population','The noun commonly takes “of” for the people being dominated.'),
q('What outcome most directly counters subjugation?','The population regains political independence.','The ruling army changes its uniform.','A historian writes a longer account.','Independence removes the condition of forced control.'),
q('Which word is the related verb? “The records describe the ___ of the region.”','subjugation','subjugate','subjugatingly','The sentence needs a noun after “the”; subjugation names the process.')
]);
P('sublimate','From Latin sublimare “raise or elevate,” from sublimis “high.” In chemistry it came to describe a solid changing directly to vapor; psychology extended the sense to redirecting an impulse.','Latin sublimare “raise, elevate”','Under low pressure, the dry ice will sublimate without forming a liquid pool.','在低壓下，乾冰會直接升華，不會形成液態水池。','chemistry','Verb: a solid sublimates; a person can sublimate an impulse into activity.','This context uses the physical change of state; dry ice goes directly from solid carbon dioxide to gas.',M('The ice sublimated into a puddle before evaporating.','The ice melted into a puddle before evaporating.','A puddle shows a liquid phase; sublimation skips the liquid and passes from solid to gas.','distinction'),[
q('What happens when dry ice sublimates?','It changes from solid directly to gas.','It changes from solid to liquid and remains there.','It changes from gas directly to solid.','Sublimation skips the liquid state.'),
q('Which sentence uses the psychological sense of “sublimate”?','She sublimated her anger into focused training.','She sublimated the metal by filing it into a sheet.','She sublimated the meeting onto the calendar.','The psychological sense is to channel an impulse into another activity.'),
q('Which change is not sublimation?','A solid melts into a liquid.','A solid changes directly into vapor.','A solid dry-ice block becomes carbon dioxide gas.','Melting creates a liquid phase, whereas sublimation bypasses it.'),
q('In the lab note “the sample sublimated under vacuum,” why is low pressure relevant?','It can allow the solid to pass directly into vapor.','It proves the sample became a liquid first.','It means the sample was chemically neutral.','The verb describes direct solid-to-vapor change, which can occur under reduced pressure.')
]);
P('sublimation','From Latin sublimationem, from sublimare “raise or elevate.” The scientific sense names a direct solid-to-vapor phase change; psychology uses it for redirected impulse.','Latin sublimationem, from sublimare “raise”','Sublimation left a thin layer of frost crystals on the cold surface.','升華作用在冷表面留下了一層薄薄的霜晶。','chemistry','Noun: sublimation of a solid; psychological sublimation of an impulse.','The scientific sense is a phase change that bypasses liquid; the psychological sense is redirection.',M('Sublimation turned the ice into liquid water before it evaporated.','Melting turned the ice into liquid water before it evaporated.','A liquid intermediate is melting followed by evaporation, not sublimation.','distinction'),[
q('Which observation demonstrates sublimation?','A solid becomes vapor without first becoming liquid.','A solid melts into a puddle.','A vapor condenses into droplets.','Sublimation is the solid-to-gas transition that skips liquid.'),
q('In psychology, what is sublimation?','Redirecting an impulse into a different activity.','Suppressing all memory of an event.','Measuring a substance’s boiling point.','The psychological term refers to channeling a drive into another activity.'),
q('Which is the best contrast between melting and sublimation?','Melting forms a liquid; sublimation bypasses it.','Melting bypasses liquid; sublimation forms it.','Both always turn liquid into solid.','Only sublimation skips the liquid phase.'),
q('A chemist warms a solid and observes vapor with no liquid residue. Which process is named?','Sublimation.','Condensation.','Freezing.','Direct transition from solid to vapor is sublimation.')
]);
P('subliminal','From sub- “below” and Latin limen “threshold,” with -al forming an adjective: below the threshold of conscious awareness.','sub- below + Latin limen “threshold” + -al','The video flashed a subliminal image too briefly for viewers to notice consciously.','影片閃過一幅短暫到觀眾無法有意識察覺的閾下影像。','psychology','Adjective: subliminal message, cue, or influence.','It describes a stimulus below conscious perception, not one that is merely secret or subtle.',M('The subliminal announcement was clearly read aloud and discussed by everyone.','The announcement was explicit and consciously noticed by everyone.','Something clearly noticed and discussed is not below the threshold of awareness.','distinction'),[
q('What makes a cue subliminal in the usual psychological sense?','It is too faint or brief for conscious notice.','It is printed in large letters for everyone to read.','It is a deliberate spoken instruction.','A subliminal stimulus falls below conscious perception.'),
q('Which example best fits “subliminal”?','A single-frame image viewers do not consciously register.','A billboard that commuters read aloud.','A warning announced over a loudspeaker.','The image is too brief to notice on purpose.'),
q('Why is “secret” not an exact synonym for “subliminal”?','A secret can be consciously known by some people.','A subliminal cue must be written in code.','A secret is always shorter than a frame.','Subliminal specifically concerns the threshold of conscious perception, not secrecy.'),
q('Which modifier naturally describes a message presented below conscious awareness?','subliminal','submarine','substantial','Subliminal is the adjective for an imperceptible or barely perceived cue.')
]);
P('subliminally','Formed from subliminal + -ly. Subliminal combines sub- “below” with Latin limen “threshold.”','subliminal + -ly; subliminal from sub- + Latin limen “threshold”','The logo was subliminally present in the scene, barely registering with viewers.','標誌以閾下方式出現在場景中，觀眾幾乎沒有察覺。','psychology','Adverb: subliminally influence, present, or suggest.','It modifies how something is perceived or conveyed, below the level of conscious awareness.',M('The slogan was subliminally obvious because it filled the screen for ten seconds.','The slogan was plainly visible because it filled the screen for ten seconds.','A long, obvious display is consciously noticed, not perceived subliminally.','distinction'),[
q('If a sound affects a listener subliminally, how does it register?','Below the listener’s conscious awareness.','As a clearly understood spoken sentence.','Only after it has been translated.','The adverb means in a way below conscious perception.'),
q('Which sentence uses “subliminally” correctly?','The brief cue was subliminally perceived, if at all.','The audience subliminally applauded for five minutes.','The actor subliminally the script.','The adverb modifies “perceived” and describes below-conscious awareness.'),
q('What is the closest meaning of “subliminally suggested”?','Suggested without conscious notice.','Suggested in a very loud voice.','Suggested by a legal contract.','Subliminally means beneath the threshold of conscious perception.'),
q('Why would a ten-second title card usually not be described as subliminal?','Viewers have time to notice it consciously.','It appears below the ocean.','It contains too many letters to be read.','A conspicuous sustained display is above, not below, conscious awareness.')
]);
P('submarine','From Latin submarinus, formed from sub- “under” and marinus “of the sea.” The noun developed for a vessel that travels below the sea surface.','Latin submarinus, sub- “under” + marinus “of the sea”','The submarine remained underwater until the research vessel signaled it to surface.','研究潛艇一直待在水下，直到調查船發出上浮信號。','naval','Noun: a submarine is an underwater vessel; adjective: submarine cables lie under the sea.','The context uses the countable vessel sense; the same form can modify something under the sea.',M('The submarine sailed across the dry desert without leaving the sea.','The submarine sailed beneath the sea and later surfaced.','A submarine is a vessel designed to travel underwater; a dry desert is not its marine setting.','meaning'),[
q('A navy launches a submarine. What kind of craft is it?','A vessel able to travel beneath the sea surface.','A boat that can travel only on dry land.','A floating platform that cannot submerge.','The noun submarine names an underwater vessel.'),
q('In “submarine cable,” what does “submarine” mean?','Located or laid under the sea.','Owned by a naval officer.','Made from a ship’s engine.','The adjective sense describes something existing beneath the sea.'),
q('Which sentence uses “submarine” as a noun?','The submarine surfaced beside the research ship.','A submarine canyon cuts the seabed.','They installed a submarine cable.','In the first sentence it names the vessel; the other examples use an adjective.'),
q('What is the relationship between the adjective and noun senses?','Both concern being under the sea.','Both mean traveling above the clouds.','The noun is an instrument for measuring depth.','The adjective refers to undersea location, while the noun is a vessel designed to go there.')
]);
P('submerge','From Latin submergere, from sub- “under” + mergere “to dip, plunge.” It can also mean to cover or absorb something so it is hidden.','Latin submergere: sub- “under” + mergere “dip, plunge”','At high tide, the water will submerge the low footpath.','漲潮時，海水會淹沒那條低窪步道。','coastal science','Verb: submerge an object in water; a feeling can also be submerged beneath something.','The physical sense requires covering with liquid; the figurative sense means obscuring or absorbing.',M('The diver submerged the buoy so it floated visibly above the waves.','The diver submerged the buoy so it disappeared beneath the water.','To submerge is to put completely under water, not leave floating above it.','meaning'),[
q('If rising water submerges a road, what happens to it?','It goes completely beneath the water.','It is painted with a warning stripe.','It rises above the flood.','Submerge means to cover or place completely under water.'),
q('Which sentence uses the figurative sense of “submerge”?','Work worries submerged her excitement about the trip.','The diver submerged the camera in a tank.','The flood submerged the bridge deck.','The first sentence means worries overwhelmed or obscured her excitement.'),
q('Choose the correct form: “The flood may ___ the lower fields.”','submerge','submerged','submergingly','After modal “may,” use the base form of the verb.'),
q('Why is “submerge” more exact than “wet” for the flooded path?','It says the path was covered completely by water.','It says the path was dried by the sun.','It says the path changed color.','Submerge describes going or being put entirely beneath a liquid.')
]);
P('submerged','Past participle and adjective of submerge, from Latin submergere “plunge under.” As an adjective it can describe something underwater or hidden beneath a dominant culture.','past participle of submerge; Latin submergere “plunge under”','The crew inspected a submerged pipeline running below the harbor.','船員檢查了港口下方的一段水下管線。','engineering','Adjective: submerged + object; also submerged communities or traditions.','Here it means physically under a liquid; figurative usage means hidden beneath a dominant culture.',M('The submerged pipe remained completely dry above the waterline.','The exposed pipe remained completely dry above the waterline.','A submerged object is under the surface, so it cannot at the same time remain above the waterline.','distinction'),[
q('Where is a submerged pipeline?','Beneath the surface of a liquid.','On top of a dry hill.','Inside an aircraft cabin.','Submerged means under the liquid’s surface.'),
q('What could “a submerged tradition” mean figuratively?','A tradition hidden beneath a dominant culture.','A tradition that exists only underwater.','A tradition written in the future tense.','The figurative sense can describe something concealed beneath a dominant culture.'),
q('Which word is the base verb of “submerged”?','submerge','submersible','submergence','Submerged is the past form or participle of the verb submerge.'),
q('If divers find a submerged statue, what can they infer?','The statue is below the water’s surface.','The statue is made of soft clay.','The statue has never been seen by anyone.','The word tells its location under water; it does not specify material or history.')
]);
P('submersible','From submerse/submerge, ultimately Latin submergere “plunge under,” plus -ible “able to be.”','submerge/submerse + -ible “able to be”','The research team sent a small submersible to photograph the deep-sea vents.','研究團隊派出一艘小型潛水器拍攝深海熱泉。','oceanography','Noun: a submersible is a craft; adjective: equipment can be submersible.','The noun names a vessel able to operate under water; it is not necessarily a full-size submarine.',M('The submersible could travel underwater, but it was not submersible.','The submersible could travel underwater because it was submersible.','Calling a craft a submersible already denotes its ability to operate beneath the water surface.','word form'),[
q('A crew lowers a submersible into a deep trench. What did they lower?','A craft designed to operate underwater.','A device that can only float on a pond.','A land vehicle with no waterproofing.','A submersible is a vessel or device able to be placed or operated under water.'),
q('In “submersible camera,” what does the adjective promise?','The camera can be used beneath the water surface.','The camera records only submarines.','The camera measures temperature below freezing.','The adjective means able to operate or be placed underwater.'),
q('How does a submersible differ from “submerged”?','Submersible describes ability; submerged describes being under the surface.','Submersible describes a past event; submerged names a vessel.','They mean exactly the same state.','The -ible form marks capability, while submerged describes actual location.'),
q('Which phrase uses the noun form?','The submersible returned with images of the seabed.','The submersible hatch sealed tightly.','A submersible robot mapped the trench.','In the first sentence it is a countable craft; in the other two it modifies a noun.')
]);
P('submission','From Latin submissionem, from submittere “lower, yield,” formed from sub- “under” + mittere “send.” It denotes an act of handing in or yielding, and also a document handed in.','Latin submissionem, from submittere “lower, yield”','The journal extended the deadline for submission of revised papers.','期刊延長了修訂論文的提交期限。','publishing','Noun: submission of + work; submission to + control.','The context is the act of formally handing in work; other senses include yielding and the submitted document.',M('Her submission to the journal was accepted, so she never sent it in.','Her submission to the journal was accepted after she sent it in.','A submission to a journal is material formally handed in; acceptance follows submission.','collocation'),[
q('When does a researcher make a submission to a journal?','When formally sending a paper for consideration.','When withdrawing a paper before sending it.','When reading a journal without writing anything.','Submission is the act of formally giving work to a body for review.'),
q('In “his submission to the captor,” which sense is intended?','Acceptance of the stronger person’s control.','A document uploaded to a website.','A school subject studied for an exam.','Submission can mean yielding to someone stronger.'),
q('A conference received 120 submissions. What does that count?','Documents or proposals handed in for consideration.','The number of people who reviewed the papers.','The conference’s lecture rooms.','A submission can also be the work or proposal that is formally handed in.'),
q('Which preposition fits: “submission ___ the committee”?','to','at','from','The standard pattern is submission to a person or reviewing body.')
]);
P('submissive','From submit + -ive; submit traces to Latin submittere “lower, yield.” The adjective describes willingness to obey without argument.','submit + -ive; submit from Latin submittere “lower, yield”','The usually outspoken dog became submissive when the trainer gave a calm command.','平時很愛吠的狗在訓練師平靜地下令時變得順從。','animal behavior','Adjective: submissive to a person or authority; a submissive response.','It describes a disposition to obey, not simply politeness or quietness.',M('The submissive witness refused every instruction and insisted on acting alone.','The defiant witness refused every instruction and insisted on acting alone.','Refusing instructions and insisting on independence describe defiance, the opposite of submissiveness.','distinction'),[
q('What behavior is most clearly submissive?','Obeying another person without arguing.','Rejecting every request to show independence.','Giving orders to a whole team.','Submissive means willing to obey what others want.'),
q('Which sentence uses the adjective correctly?','The dog remained submissive to its handler.','The dog submissive the handler’s command.','The handler praised submissively dog.','“Submissive” is an adjective and “submissive to” is a natural construction.'),
q('What is the opposite tendency of a submissive response?','Defiant resistance.','Quiet agreement.','Careful listening.','Submissive describes obedience; defiance is resistance.'),
q('Why might “quiet” alone fail to mean “submissive”?','A quiet person may still refuse to obey.','Quiet always means eager to obey.','Submissive refers only to volume.','Submissiveness concerns obedience, while quietness concerns sound or talk.')
]);
P('submit','From Latin submittere “lower, yield,” from sub- “under” + mittere “send.” Modern uses include handing in work and yielding to authority.','Latin submittere: sub- “under” + mittere “send”','Please submit the completed application by Friday at noon.','請在星期五中午前提交填妥的申請表。','administration','Verb: submit a form/work to a person or body; submit to authority.','The example uses the transitive “hand in” sense; submit to can mean accept another’s control.',M('Please submit to the committee your application by Friday was the form.','Please submit your application to the committee by Friday.','The original combines two word orders; place the object after submit and use “to” for the recipient.','grammar'),[
q('What does a student do when asked to submit an essay?','Hand it in for evaluation.','Keep it private and never send it.','Rewrite it as a spoken command.','Submit means give work so it can be judged.'),
q('In “The smaller army submitted to the invader,” what happened?','It accepted the invader’s control.','It delivered a written report.','It won the battle and ruled the invader.','Submit to can mean yield to a person or situation.'),
q('Choose the correct construction: “They submitted ___ the panel.”','the proposal to','to the proposal','the panel for','The direct object is the proposal; “to the panel” identifies the recipient.'),
q('Which form follows “must” in “Applicants must ___ two references”?','submit','submitted','submission','A modal verb takes the base form “submit.”')
]);
P('suborder','Formed from sub- “below” + order. In biological classification, a suborder is a rank below an order and above a family.','sub- “below” + order','The museum’s label places this fossil in the suborder Theropoda.','博物館的標籤將這件化石歸入獸腳亞目。','biology','Noun: a suborder within an order in taxonomy.','It names a taxonomic rank beneath an order, not a command or a separate unrelated order.',M('The species belongs to a suborder above its biological order.','The species belongs to a suborder below its biological order.','A suborder is classified under an order, not above it.','meaning'),[
q('Where does a suborder sit in biological classification?','Below an order.','Above a class.','Outside every taxonomic group.','The prefix sub- means below; a suborder is a group under an order.'),
q('Which chain has the correct rank relationship?','Order → suborder → family.','Suborder → order → class.','Family → suborder → species.','A suborder is below an order and above a family.'),
q('In “The fossil’s suborder includes several families,” what does the word name?','A taxonomic grouping beneath an order.','A command given to a fossil.','A group of organisms above a class.','The biological sense is a rank lower than order.'),
q('Why is “suborder” not simply another word for “order”?','It marks a more specific rank beneath an order.','It means an instruction written in smaller letters.','It names the largest biological grouping.','The prefix signals a subordinate taxonomic level.')
]);
P('subordinate','From Latin subordinatus, past participle of subordinare “place in a lower rank,” from sub- “under” + ordinare “arrange.”','Latin subordinatus, from subordinare “place in lower rank”','The assistant is subordinate to the department head but manages the daily schedule.','這名助理職級低於部門主管，但負責管理日常行程。','workplace','Adjective: subordinate to a person; noun: a person lower in rank; verb: subordinate one goal to another.','This sentence uses the rank adjective; the word also names a lower-ranking person and can mean treat something as less important.',M('The junior analyst is subordinate than the director.','The junior analyst is subordinate to the director.','The adjective takes “to” for the higher authority, not “than.”','collocation'),[
q('If a role is subordinate to the director’s role, how does it rank?','It is lower in rank.','It is more senior.','It is unrelated to the organization.','Subordinate means lower in rank or importance.'),
q('In “Her subordinate prepared the report,” what does the noun name?','A person with a lower position.','The organization’s chief executive.','A report’s subject matter.','A subordinate can be a person who reports to someone higher.'),
q('What does “subordinate personal ambition to public duty” mean?','Treat public duty as more important than personal ambition.','Make public duty answer to personal ambition.','Remove public duty from consideration.','The verb can mean place one concern below another in importance.'),
q('Which phrase correctly expresses rank?','subordinate to the supervisor','subordinate than the supervisor','subordinate with the supervisor','The conventional construction is “subordinate to.”')
]);
P('subordinating','From subordinate and -ing; in grammar, a subordinating conjunction introduces a dependent clause.','subordinate + -ing; related to Latin subordinare “rank under”','“Although” is a subordinating conjunction in “Although the road was wet, the race continued.”','在「雖然道路濕滑，賽事仍繼續」中，although 是從屬連接詞。','grammar','Adjective: subordinating conjunction; present participle of subordinate.','The grammar label applies to a conjunction that links a dependent clause to a main clause.',M('“Although” is subordinating in “Although it rained, we left,” because it introduces the main clause.','“Although” is subordinating in “Although it rained, we left,” because it introduces the dependent clause.','“Although it rained” cannot stand as the main clause here; the conjunction introduces a dependent clause.','grammar'),[
q('What does a subordinating conjunction introduce?','A dependent clause.','A list of unrelated nouns.','A question with no verb.','It links a dependent clause to a main clause.'),
q('In “Because the bus was late, we walked,” which word is subordinating?','Because','Bus','Walked','“Because” introduces the dependent reason clause.'),
q('Which clause is dependent in “Although the lights failed, the show continued”?','Although the lights failed.','The show continued.','Both clauses are independent.','The subordinating conjunction although makes the first clause dependent.'),
q('Why is “and” not subordinating in “Mia sang and Leo danced”?','It coordinates two clauses rather than making one dependent.','It introduces a reason clause.','It marks a condition that has not happened.','“And” is coordinating; it joins elements of equal grammatical status.')
]);
P('suborn','From Latin subornare “furnish secretly, instigate,” from sub- “underhandedly” + ornare “equip.” The modern legal use is to induce wrongdoing such as perjury.','Latin subornare “furnish secretly, instigate”','The prosecutor alleged that the witness had been suborned to give false testimony.','檢察官指控該證人受人唆使而作偽證。','law','Verb: suborn a witness to lie or commit an unlawful act.','The verb means induce or procure wrongdoing; it does not mean simply question or summon a witness.',M('The lawyer suborned the witness to tell the truth under oath.','The lawyer coached the witness to tell the truth under oath.','Suborn means induce someone to lie or commit wrongdoing, not encourage truthful testimony.','meaning'),[
q('A person who suborns a witness tries to make the witness do what?','Lie in court or commit another unlawful act.','Give accurate testimony voluntarily.','Serve as a juror.','Suborn specifically means induce a witness to lie or otherwise act unlawfully.'),
q('Which verb belongs in a legal report: “The official was accused of ___ a witness”?','suborning','subordinating','submerging','Suborning is the act of inducing a witness to lie or commit an unlawful act.'),
q('Why is “suborn” more specific than “persuade”?','It involves inducing wrongdoing, often perjury.','It means to persuade someone to buy a magazine.','It describes lowering water below a surface.','The legal sense of suborn carries the unlawful purpose.'),
q('If the witness was suborned, what does the passive form imply?','Someone induced the witness to commit wrongdoing.','The witness was promoted to judge.','The witness was asked a neutral question.','Suborned is the past participle of a verb meaning procure unlawful conduct.')
]);
P('subscribe','From Latin subscribere “write underneath, sign,” from sub- “under” + scribere “write.” Later uses include paying regularly, pledging money, and agreeing with a view.','Latin subscribere: sub- “under” + scribere “write”','We subscribe to the journal so the laboratory receives each monthly issue.','我們訂閱這本期刊，讓實驗室每月都能收到一期。','publishing','Verb: subscribe to a publication/service, subscribe money/shares, or subscribe to a belief.','The example uses regular paid delivery; the verb can also mean pledge funds or agree with an opinion.',M('We subscribe the journal to receive each issue.','We subscribe to the journal to receive each issue.','The publication sense takes “to” before the service or publication.','collocation'),[
q('What does a family do when it subscribes to a magazine?','Pays regularly to receive its issues.','Writes a review without receiving it.','Cancels every future issue.','To subscribe to a publication is to pay for regular delivery.'),
q('In “She subscribed $500 to the relief fund,” what did she do?','Promised or contributed money.','Bought a monthly magazine.','Disagreed with the fund’s goal.','Subscribe can mean pledge money to a cause.'),
q('What does “I subscribe to the view that evidence matters” mean?','I agree with that opinion.','I receive the view in the mail.','I pay to remove the view.','Subscribe to a view means accept or agree with it.'),
q('Which construction is correct for the service sense?','subscribe to the online archive','subscribe the online archive','subscribe at the online archive','The usual pattern is subscribe to a publication or service.')
]);
P('subscription','From Latin subscriptionem, from subscribere “write beneath, sign.” The modern noun commonly refers to payment for regular access or delivery.','Latin subscriptionem, from subscribere “write beneath, sign”','Her annual subscription gives her access to the journal’s complete digital archive.','她的年度訂閱讓她可以使用該期刊的完整數位資料庫。','publishing','Noun: subscription to a service or magazine; a subscription is also the payment or plan.','It names the paid arrangement, not each issue itself.',M('My subscription arrived in the mail as a single magazine issue.','The magazine issue arrived in the mail under my subscription.','A subscription is the paid arrangement; the individual issue is what it provides.','word-choice'),[
q('What does an annual software subscription usually provide?','Access to the software for a paid period.','Permanent ownership with no time limit.','A printed instruction manual only.','A subscription is payment for service access over a period.'),
q('Which noun names the arrangement, rather than a single issue?','subscription','article','envelope','The subscription is the paid plan that supplies issues or access.'),
q('Which phrase is standard?','a subscription to the journal','a subscription at the journal','a subscription from reading','Use “subscription to” for the publication or service.'),
q('Why might a customer renew a subscription?','To continue receiving access or issues after the paid term.','To return a single article to its writer.','To change the publication into a library.','Renewal extends the paid access or delivery arrangement.')
]);
P('subsection','Formed from sub- “under, below” + section, from Latin sectio “a cutting.”','sub- “below” + section, from Latin sectio “cutting”','The safety rules appear in subsection 4.2 of the handbook.','安全規則列在手冊第 4.2 小節。','publishing','Noun: a subsection is a division within a larger section.','Numbering such as 4.2 shows that the subsection sits inside section 4.',M('The handbook’s subsection 4.2 contains the whole book and all its chapters.','The handbook’s subsection 4.2 contains one smaller division within a section.','A subsection is a subdivision inside a larger section, not an entire book.','meaning'),[
q('Where would readers find a subsection?','Inside a larger section.','Before the title page of every book.','Only in a book’s index.','A subsection is a numbered or titled division within a section.'),
q('What does a label such as “5.3.1” often identify?','A subsection nested within a larger numbered section.','The book’s publication year.','A page’s exact height.','Nested numbering commonly marks increasingly specific subsections.'),
q('Which outline shows a subsection correctly?','Section 2 → subsection 2.1 → paragraph','Subsection 2.1 → whole book → section 2','Index → subsection 2.1 → title page','The subsection is contained within the parent section.'),
q('Why is “subsection” more precise than “section” for 4.2?','It signals a smaller division inside a larger section.','It signals a new, unrelated book.','It signals a spoken instruction.','The prefix sub- marks a lower-level division.')
]);
P('subsequent','From Latin subsequentem, present participle of subsequi “follow closely,” from sub- + sequi “follow.”','Latin subsequentem, from subsequi “follow closely”','The subsequent chapter explains how the trial changed the law.','後續章節說明了這場審判如何改變法律。','publishing','Adjective: subsequent event, stage, or chapter; subsequent to + event.','It means coming after in time or sequence; it can also describe a next stage resulting from an earlier one.',M('The subsequent chapter appeared before the opening chapter.','The preceding chapter appeared before the opening chapter.','Subsequent means coming after, so it cannot describe a chapter that appeared earlier.','distinction'),[
q('A subsequent meeting takes place when?','After an earlier meeting.','Before the first meeting.','At the same time as every meeting.','Subsequent means coming after something in time.'),
q('Which event is subsequent to the vote?','The ceremony held the next day.','The debate held the previous week.','The discussion happening during the vote.','The next-day ceremony follows the vote.'),
q('What can “subsequent damage” mean in a report?','Damage that followed as a result or next stage.','Damage that existed before any event.','Damage unrelated in time or sequence.','Subsequent can describe what follows as a consequence or next stage.'),
q('Which phrase is standard?','subsequent to the inspection','subsequent than the inspection','subsequent with inspecting','The adjective commonly takes “to” before the earlier event.')
]);
P('subsequently','Formed from subsequent + -ly; subsequent comes from Latin subsequi “follow closely.”','subsequent + -ly; from Latin subsequi “follow closely”','The bridge was closed after the inspection and subsequently reopened the next morning.','橋梁檢查後暫時封閉，隨後於隔天早上重新開放。','transportation','Adverb: subsequently describes a later event or action.','It points back to an event already mentioned and places the next event later in time.',M('The bridge subsequently reopened before it was first closed.','The bridge reopened before it was later closed.','Subsequently means later than the event already mentioned, not earlier than it.','usage'),[
q('If the team subsequently changed its plan, when did the change happen?','After an earlier event or decision.','Before the plan was ever made.','At a time unrelated to the account.','Subsequently marks a later point in the sequence.'),
q('Choose the sentence that uses “subsequently” correctly.','The alarm sounded; the building was subsequently evacuated.','The building was subsequently evacuated before the alarm sounded.','The alarm subsequently to the building.','The evacuation follows the alarm, matching the adverb’s time relation.'),
q('Which adverb is closest in meaning?','later','earlier','simultaneously','Subsequently means at a later time.'),
q('Why might a report say the suspect was arrested and subsequently released?','Release happened after the arrest.','Release happened before the arrest.','The two events were identical.','The adverb marks the second event as later than the first.')
]);
P('subside','From Latin subsidere “sit down, settle,” from sub- “under” + sedere “sit.” It means to become weaker or sink to a lower level.','Latin subsidere “sit down, settle”','The floodwater began to subside after the storm moved inland.','風暴移往內陸後，洪水開始退去。','weather','Verb: a storm, pain, or excitement subsides; water or ground may sink.','This example uses the lessening/lowering water sense; the word also describes emotions or symptoms becoming calmer.',M('The floodwater subsided and rose several meters higher.','The floodwater subsided and fell several meters.','To subside is to sink or become lower, not rise higher.','meaning'),[
q('What happens when a storm subsides?','It becomes weaker and calmer.','It grows steadily more violent.','It changes into a permanent building.','Subside means to weaken after a peak.'),
q('Which sentence uses the sinking sense of “subside”?','The ground subsided beneath the old road.','Her anger subsided after an apology.','The applause subsided as the lights dimmed.','The ground sentence describes land sinking to a lower level.'),
q('What would a doctor mean by “the swelling should subside”?','It should become smaller.','It should spread to new areas.','It should become a bone.','A symptom that subsides becomes weaker or less pronounced.'),
q('Why is “The lake subsided below its banks” understandable?','The water level sank to a lower level.','The lake became more excited.','The lake’s color became brighter.','Subside can describe water or ground sinking.')
]);
P('subsidiary','From Latin subsidiarius “serving as a reserve or support,” from subsidium “assistance, reserve force.” As a noun it commonly means a company controlled by another.','Latin subsidiarius, from subsidium “assistance, reserve force”','The bank’s regional subsidiary handles small-business loans in the north.','該銀行的北方區域子公司負責小型企業貸款。','business','Noun: a subsidiary is a company controlled by another; adjective: subsidiary support is secondary or additional.','The context uses the company sense; the adjective can mean providing extra help or being less important.',M('The parent company is a subsidiary owned by its smaller branch.','The smaller branch is a subsidiary owned by the parent company.','A subsidiary is controlled by a parent company, not the other way around in this description.','meaning'),[
q('A company owns most of another firm. The second firm is its what?','subsidiary','competitor','creditor','A subsidiary is controlled by another company.'),
q('In “a subsidiary concern,” what does the adjective mean?','Secondary or providing extra support.','The only important issue.','Located below the soil.','Subsidiary as an adjective means extra or less central.'),
q('Which sentence uses the noun sense?','The manufacturer opened a subsidiary in Chile.','The report included a subsidiary note.','That was a subsidiary concern.','The first sentence uses subsidiary as a company.'),
q('What relationship connects a parent company and its subsidiary?','The parent controls the subsidiary.','The subsidiary always controls its parent.','They must be separate competitors.','A subsidiary belongs to or is controlled by a larger company.')
]);
P('subsidise','British spelling variant of subsidize, formed from subsidy + -ise; subsidy traces to Latin subsidium “assistance.”','subsidy + -ise; subsidy from Latin subsidium “assistance”','The city will subsidise bus fares for students from low-income families.','市府將補貼低收入家庭學生的公車票價。','public policy','British verb spelling: subsidise costs or a service with financial support.','It means pay part of a cost so that users pay less; American English commonly spells the verb subsidize.',M('The council will subsidise fares by charging every passenger the full increased price.','The council will subsidise fares by paying part of the cost.','Subsidising reduces the user’s cost through financial support; charging the full increased fare does not.','meaning'),[
q('What does the city do when it subsidises bus fares?','Pays part of the cost so passengers pay less.','Raises every fare to cover the full cost.','Stops the buses from running.','To subsidise a cost is to support it with money.'),
q('Which spelling is the British form?','subsidise','subsidizee','subsydise','British English uses -ise in subsidise; -ize is common in American English.'),
q('Who may subsidise a service?','A government or private organization providing financial support.','Only the customers receiving the service.','Only a court issuing a verdict.','Subsidies can be provided by public or private funds.'),
q('Why might a town subsidise a rural bus route?','To keep fares lower or help the service continue.','To make the route disappear immediately.','To ensure no one can use the bus.','Financial support can lower costs and sustain a service.')
]);
P('subsidize','From subsidy + -ize; subsidy comes from Latin subsidium “assistance, reserve force.”','subsidy + -ize; subsidy from Latin subsidium “assistance”','The grant will subsidize insulation upgrades in older apartment buildings.','這筆補助將資助老舊公寓的隔熱改善工程。','public policy','Verb: subsidize a cost/activity with money; US spelling with -ize.','It means provide money toward a cost; the source entry also includes paying part of an activity or research cost.',M('The grant subsidized the project by refusing to contribute any money.','The grant subsidized the project by paying part of its cost.','Subsidize means give financial support; contributing no money cannot subsidize a project.','meaning'),[
q('If a grant subsidizes a project, what does it do?','Pays part of the project’s cost.','Bans the project from receiving money.','Measures the project’s temperature.','To subsidize is to give money to help pay for something.'),
q('Which use matches the activity/research sense?','A foundation subsidized the field study’s expenses.','The researcher subscribed to a journal.','The lab submerged its equipment.','Paying part of the research cost is to subsidize the study.'),
q('Which spelling is standard in American English?','subsidize','subsidise','subsidizee','American English commonly uses the -ize spelling.'),
q('A city subsidizes a rural bus route. What may this achieve?','The user may pay less and the service may continue.','The service must become more expensive.','The service is automatically closed.','Subsidizing pays part of an activity’s cost and can help it continue.',2)
]);
P('subsidy','From French subsidie, from Latin subsidium “assistance, reserve force.”','French subsidie, from Latin subsidium “assistance”','A housing subsidy helped the family afford rent near the school.','住房補助幫助這個家庭負擔學校附近的租金。','public policy','Noun: a subsidy is money paid to lower a cost or keep an activity going.','The source entry includes government payments and broader financial help that sustains an activity.',M('The subsidy raised the rent by removing all financial support.','The subsidy helped lower the rent by providing financial support.','A subsidy is financial help intended to lower a cost or support an activity.','meaning'),[
q('What is a government housing subsidy?','Financial help that lowers a housing cost.','A tax penalty for renting a home.','A lease that forbids public assistance.','A subsidy is money paid to reduce a cost.'),
q('What may a subsidy do for a research project?','Help pay its expenses so the work can continue.','Require it to stop collecting data.','Convert its results into a law.','Subsidy can mean financial help that allows an activity to continue.'),
q('Who commonly provides a subsidy?','A government or funding organization.','Only the person who receives the subsidy.','A dictionary defining a word.','Public or private bodies may provide financial support.'),
q('How does a subsidy differ from a loan?','A subsidy is financial support, not necessarily money that must be repaid.','A subsidy is always a written promise to repay with interest.','A subsidy is an unpaid invoice.','The word denotes money paid to lower costs; repayment is not part of its basic meaning.')
]);
P('subsist','From Latin subsistere “stand firm, remain,” from sub- + sistere “cause to stand.” Modern use often refers to surviving on little.','Latin subsistere “stand firm, remain”','During the drought, the village subsisted on stored grain and collected rainwater.','乾旱期間，村民靠儲存的穀物和收集的雨水勉強維生。','survival','Verb: subsist on + limited food, income, or resources.','It means remain alive, especially with very little; “on” introduces the means of survival.',M('The family subsisted on no food or water for months and remained healthy.','The family struggled to subsist on very little food and water.','Subsist means stay alive, often on limited resources; no food or water for months is not a plausible account.','meaning'),[
q('A family subsists on a small pension. What does that imply?','The pension is their limited means of staying alive.','They spend the pension on luxuries only.','They have stopped living together.','Subsist often means survive on very limited income or food.'),
q('Which preposition completes the phrase? “They subsisted ___ beans and rice.”','on','under','between','The standard construction is subsist on a source of food or income.'),
q('Which situation best fits “subsist”?','A remote crew stays alive on stored food.','A company expands its luxury offices.','A lake freezes into solid ice.','Subsist describes remaining alive, often with limited resources.'),
q('Why is “subsist” stronger than “eat” in the report?','It emphasizes survival on restricted resources.','It means to prepare a feast.','It describes a type of soil.','The verb highlights basic survival rather than the general act of eating.')
]);
P('subsistence','From Latin subsistentia, from subsistere “stand firm, remain.” It refers to barely staying alive and to the means that sustain life.','Latin subsistentia, from subsistere “remain”','The farmers grew rice mainly for subsistence rather than for export.','農民種植稻米主要是為了糊口，而不是出口。','agriculture','Noun/adjective: subsistence farming or a means of subsistence; just enough for survival.','The context uses the livelihood sense; the adjective often describes production that provides only basic needs.',M('The family’s subsistence income paid for several luxury yachts and a mansion.','The family’s substantial income paid for several luxury yachts and a mansion.','Subsistence describes only enough resources to stay alive, not wealth for many luxuries.','distinction'),[
q('What is subsistence farming primarily intended to do?','Provide enough food for the farmer’s basic needs.','Produce luxury crops only for global export.','Replace all forms of agriculture.','Subsistence production is mainly for staying alive, not commercial surplus.'),
q('In “Fishing was their means of subsistence,” what is fishing?','The means by which they stayed alive.','A recreational activity unrelated to food or income.','A type of government payment.','Subsistence can mean the means by which a person stays alive.'),
q('Which word contrasts with a subsistence wage?','A high income beyond basic survival needs.','A salary barely covering food and shelter.','A seasonal crop used for household meals.','Subsistence indicates only basic survival, so a high surplus contrasts with it.'),
q('Why might a report call a village’s economy subsistence-based?','Most production supports residents’ basic survival.','All goods are luxury exports.','Residents receive no food or income.','Subsistence means livelihood at a basic level, often with little surplus.')
]);
P('subsoil','Formed from sub- “below” + soil; it names the layer beneath topsoil.','sub- “below” + soil','Roots of the deep-rooted trees reached moisture in the subsoil.','深根樹木的根系深入底土，吸收其中的水分。','agriculture','Noun: subsoil lies beneath topsoil and often contains less organic matter.','The word identifies a soil layer by its position, not a kind of surface fertilizer.',M('The gardener spread compost through the subsoil on top of the lawn.','The gardener spread compost through the topsoil on top of the lawn.','Subsoil is beneath the topsoil; compost spread on the surface belongs in or on the topsoil.','meaning'),[
q('Where is subsoil found?','Beneath the topsoil.','Above the leaves of a plant.','Only inside a seed.','The term names soil lying under the topsoil layer.'),
q('Why may subsoil contain less organic matter than topsoil?','It lies deeper, below the surface layer where much organic material accumulates.','It is always made of pure metal.','It is exposed to more fallen leaves.','Subsoil lies under topsoil, which generally receives more surface organic matter.'),
q('Which gardener is working with subsoil?','One examining the layer uncovered beneath the topsoil.','One scraping fallen leaves from the lawn surface.','One pruning branches in a tree crown.','Subsoil is the deeper layer revealed after topsoil is removed.'),
q('What does the prefix in “subsoil” indicate?','A layer below the surface soil.','A soil that has no minerals.','A soil mixed with seawater.','Sub- means under or below.')
]);
P('subspecies','Formed from sub- “under, below” + Latin species “kind, appearance.” In taxonomy it names a distinct population within a species.','sub- “below” + Latin species “kind”','Researchers compared the island subspecies with mainland populations of the same bird.','研究人員比較了該島亞種與同一鳥種的陸地族群。','biology','Noun: a subspecies is a population within a species, often geographically or morphologically distinct.','It is a taxonomic grouping below species, not a separate genus.',M('The island subspecies is a completely unrelated order above the bird species.','The island subspecies is a distinct population within the bird species.','A subspecies belongs within a species; it is not an unrelated order above it.','meaning'),[
q('What is a subspecies?','A distinct population within a species.','A taxonomic rank above a kingdom.','A different word for an individual animal.','A subspecies is a recognizable population inside a species.'),
q('Why might island animals form a subspecies?','Geographic isolation can make the population distinct.','The island is always warmer than the mainland.','Every animal has a different name.','Subspecies may be differentiated geographically or morphologically.'),
q('Which hierarchy is correct?','Species → subspecies → local population.','Subspecies → kingdom → species.','Family → subspecies → order.','A subspecies is a subdivision within a species.'),
q('Does “subspecies” necessarily mean an entirely separate species?','No; it is a recognized population within a species.','Yes; it always means a different genus.','Yes; it means an individual specimen.','The prefix marks a lower grouping within the species.')
]);
P('substance','From Latin substantia “being, essence,” from substare “stand firm or under.” English uses include a material, the main ideas, and real importance.','Latin substantia, from substare “stand firm”','The lab stored the unknown substance in a sealed glass vial.','實驗室將這種未知物質存放在密封玻璃瓶中。','chemistry','Noun sense 1: a particular solid, liquid, or gas; also the main ideas or importance of something.','Here it means a material; substance can also mean the essential content or truth of a matter.',M('The chemist identified the substance as a paragraph’s main idea.','The chemist identified the substance as a clear liquid.','A paragraph has main ideas, while a chemical substance is a kind of material.','distinction'),[
q('A chemist labels an unknown substance. What is being labeled?','A particular material such as a solid, liquid, or gas.','A paragraph’s punctuation.','A company’s subordinate branch.','Substance can name a kind of physical material.'),
q('In “The substance of her argument was fairness,” what does substance mean?','The argument’s main idea.','A liquid inside a bottle.','Its spoken volume.','This figurative sense means the essential point or content.'),
q('If a claim has substance, what does it have?','Real importance or truth worth taking seriously.','A guaranteed chemical formula.','No supporting meaning at all.','Substance can refer to truth and importance.'),
q('Which sentence uses the material sense?','The substance reacted with water.','The substance of the speech was equality.','His proposal lacked substance.','A substance that reacts with water is a physical material.')
]);
P('substantial','From Latin substantialis, from substantia “essence, being.” It describes a large amount or something important enough to have a real effect.','Latin substantialis, from substantia “essence, being”','The bridge requires substantial repairs after the storm.','暴風雨過後，這座橋需要大規模修繕。','engineering','Adjective: substantial repairs/amount; substantial evidence or change can have real importance or effect.','This example means large in extent; another sense means important or materially consequential.',M('The bridge needs substantial repairs, just one loose bolt and a dab of paint.','The bridge needs minor repairs, just one loose bolt and a dab of paint.','A single loose bolt and a little paint are minor, not substantial repairs.','distinction'),[
q('A substantial increase in costs is what kind of increase?','Large enough to matter.','Too small to notice.','Purely imaginary.','Substantial means large in amount or enough to have an effect.'),
q('Which is a substantial change to a bridge?','Replacing several failing supports.','Changing one label on a map.','Wiping dust from a sign.','Substantial can mean important enough to make a real effect.'),
q('What does “substantial evidence” suggest?','Evidence strong or important enough to matter.','Evidence that is physically heavy only.','Evidence with no bearing on the case.','The adjective also means having real importance or effect.'),
q('Which phrase best fits a large amount?','a substantial sum','a substantial whisper','a substantial yesterday','Substantial naturally describes a significant amount of money.')
]);
P('substantially','Formed from substantial + -ly; substantial comes from Latin substantialis, from substantia “essence.”','substantial + -ly; substantial from Latin substantialis','The redesign substantially reduced the building’s annual energy use.','重新設計大幅降低了建築物的年度能源使用量。','engineering','Adverb: substantially reduce/increase; substantially similar means alike in the important points.','It can mean by a large amount or in essential points despite small differences.',M('The redesign substantially reduced energy use by exactly zero percent.','The redesign did not reduce energy use; it remained unchanged.','A zero-percent reduction is no reduction, whereas substantially indicates a large or meaningful amount.','meaning'),[
q('If energy use fell substantially, how large was the change?','Large enough to matter.','Exactly zero.','Too small to measure.','Substantially can mean by a large amount.'),
q('Two plans are substantially similar. What is alike?','Their important points, though small details may differ.','Every word and mark with no differences.','Only their paper color.','The adverb can mean alike in the essential points.'),
q('Which sentence uses “substantially” to mean “by a large amount”?','The new pump substantially lowered water use.','The contracts were substantially identical except for one date.','Substantially, the paper has a blue cover.','The first describes a large reduction; the second means similar in main points.'),
q('Why is “substantially unchanged” not necessarily identical to “exactly unchanged”?','It may allow small differences while saying the important parts stayed the same.','It means everything changed completely.','It means no one measured the item.','Substantially often concerns what is true in the important respects, allowing minor differences.')
]);
P('substantiate','From substance + -iate, ultimately from Latin substantia “being, essence”; the verb means give factual support or substance to a claim.','substance + -iate; substance from Latin substantia “being”','The audit documents substantiate the company’s claim about its emissions.','稽核文件證實了該公司對排放量所作的聲明。','business','Verb: substantiate a claim, allegation, or account with evidence.','It means prove or support a statement with facts or documents, not merely repeat it.',M('The company substantiated its claim by offering no records or evidence.','The company stated its claim but did not substantiate it with evidence.','To substantiate is to support a claim with facts; offering no evidence does not do so.','meaning'),[
q('What does a document do when it substantiates a claim?','Provides facts or evidence supporting it.','Repeats it without checking.','Removes it from a report.','Substantiate means prove or support with facts or documents.'),
q('Which evidence could substantiate a reported payment?','A dated bank record showing the transfer.','A rumor with no named source.','A blank page.','A verifiable bank record provides factual support.'),
q('Choose the correct verb: “The witness’s account helped ___ the allegation.”','substantiate','substantial','substance','The sentence needs the verb meaning support with evidence.'),
q('Why is “substantiate” stronger than “mention”?','It indicates evidence is used to support a statement.','It means to speak more loudly.','It names a company branch.','Substantiation requires support, not simply saying something.')
]);
P('substantive','From Latin substantivus, from substantia “essence, being.” It describes something important in itself and, in grammar, a word used as a noun.','Latin substantivus, from substantia “essence”','The parties reached a substantive agreement on the amount of compensation.','雙方就賠償金額達成了實質性的協議。','law','Adjective: substantive issue/change; grammar: substantive is a noun or noun-like word.','The context means dealing with an important basic matter rather than minor details; the source also has a grammatical sense.',M('The court held a substantive hearing about the main legal issue, but no actual issue was discussed.','The court held a procedural hearing about scheduling, not the substantive issue.','Substantive concerns the actual important issue; scheduling alone is procedural.','distinction'),[
q('A substantive reform changes what?','An important basic feature of the system.','Only the color of a heading.','Nothing beyond its punctuation.','Substantive means dealing with important and basic features.'),
q('In grammar, what may “a substantive” name?','A word used as a noun, including a pronoun.','A conjunction that introduces a dependent clause.','An adverb describing degree.','The grammatical sense includes words that name a person, place, or thing.'),
q('Which is a substantive issue in a contract negotiation?','The amount each party must pay.','The font size of the page number.','Whether the paper is white or cream.','Payment obligations affect the main terms, not just presentation details.'),
q('Why can a court separate substantive from procedural questions?','Substantive questions concern the rights or main issues; procedural ones concern process.','Substantive questions concern paper thickness only.','The two words both mean punctuation.','Substantive marks the actual important matter rather than the process around it.')
]);
P('substitute','From Latin substituere “put in place of,” from sub- “under/in place” + statuere “set up.” It functions as a noun or verb.','Latin substituere “put in place of”','The coach used a substitute when the starting goalkeeper was injured.','先發守門員受傷後，教練派上了一名替補。','sports','Noun: a substitute takes another’s place; verb: substitute A for B or substitute A with B.','This sentence uses the replacement person sense; the verb sense means use one thing or person in another’s place.',M('The coach substituted the injured goalkeeper for a healthy player, leaving the injured player on the field.','The coach substituted a healthy player for the injured goalkeeper.','The player introduced to take the position is substituted for the one being replaced.','collocation'),[
q('Who is a substitute goalkeeper?','A goalkeeper who takes another player’s place.','The referee who records the score.','A spectator sitting behind the goal.','A substitute is a person or thing that replaces another.'),
q('What does “substitute oat milk for dairy milk” mean?','Use oat milk in place of dairy milk.','Add dairy milk to the oat milk.','Remove both milks from the recipe.','The verb means use one thing instead of another.'),
q('Which sentence uses “substitute” as a verb?','They substituted a spare battery for the dead one.','The substitute battery was fully charged.','The substitute waited beside the bench.','In the first sentence, substituted describes the act of replacing.'),
q('Which wording correctly names the replacement?','A substitute for the injured keeper.','A substitute to the injured keeper.','A substitute than the injured keeper.','The standard noun construction is “a substitute for” someone or something.')
]);
P('substitution','From Latin substitutionem, from substituere “put in place of.”','Latin substitutionem, from substituere “put in place of”','The recipe allows the substitution of yogurt for cream.','這份食譜允許以優格代替鮮奶油。','cooking','Noun: substitution of A for B; in math, replacing one value or symbol with another.','It means putting one item in the place of another; the context shows ingredient replacement.',M('The substitution of cream for yogurt means the recipe replaces cream with yogurt.','The substitution of yogurt for cream means the recipe replaces cream with yogurt.','“Substitution of A for B” means A takes the place of B; the original reverses the items.','collocation'),[
q('What does a substitution of yogurt for cream do?','Replaces the cream with yogurt.','Adds cream but removes yogurt.','Keeps both ingredients unchanged.','A substitution puts one item in the place of another.'),
q('In algebra, what is substitution?','Replacing a variable with a value or expression.','Adding a new equation without replacing anything.','Erasing all numbers from a problem.','Substitution can mean replacing one symbol or value with another.'),
q('Which phrase clearly names the replacement ingredient?','substitution of olive oil for butter','substitution of butter by replacing olive oil for','substitution to both ingredients','The “of A for B” pattern identifies A as the replacement for B.'),
q('Why may an editor make a substitution in a sentence?','To replace a word or phrase with another one.','To move every paragraph to a new book.','To remove all meaning from the text.','A substitution is the act of putting one item in another’s place.')
]);
P('substrate','From Latin substratum, neuter past participle of substernere “spread underneath,” from sub- “under” + sternere “spread.”','Latin substratum, from substernere “spread underneath”','The enzyme binds to its substrate before converting it into a product.','酵素先與底物結合，再將底物轉化為產物。','biochemistry','Noun: a substrate may be the molecule an enzyme acts on or the material beneath a coating/organism.','This context uses the biochemical sense; in other fields it can be the underlying surface or material.',M('The enzyme converted the product into the substrate before any reaction began.','The enzyme converted the substrate into a product during the reaction.','A substrate is what the enzyme acts on; the product is what the reaction produces.','distinction'),[
q('In an enzyme reaction, what is the substrate?','The molecule the enzyme acts on.','The final product after the reaction.','The container holding the enzyme.','A substrate is converted by an enzyme into a product.'),
q('A fungus grows on a wooden substrate. What does the word mean here?','The underlying material on which it grows.','The fungus’s final food molecule.','A taxonomic rank below species.','Substrate also means the material under an organism.'),
q('Which order describes the enzyme example?','Substrate → enzyme action → product.','Product → substrate → no reaction.','Enzyme → product → substrate in every case.','The enzyme acts on the substrate to produce a product.'),
q('Why is “substrate” useful in a lab report?','It identifies the material or molecule on which a process acts.','It gives the exact color of every reaction.','It means the scientist’s conclusion.','The word names an underlying material or reaction target.')
]);
P('subsurface','Formed from sub- “below” + surface.','sub- “below” + surface','The engineers installed sensors to monitor movement in the subsurface rock.','工程師安裝感測器，以監測地表下岩層的移動。','geology','Adjective or noun modifier: subsurface rock, water, or movement exists beneath the surface.','It locates something below the surface, whether in soil, rock, or water.',M('The subsurface layer was clearly visible on top of the ground.','The surface layer was clearly visible on top of the ground.','Subsurface means below the surface, while a visible top layer is the surface layer.','distinction'),[
q('Where is subsurface water located?','Below the ground’s surface.','Only in clouds above the ground.','On top of a roof.','Subsurface describes something beneath the surface.'),
q('Which phrase uses the adjective naturally?','subsurface movement','subsurface yesterday','subsurface loudly','It modifies a noun to locate movement below the surface.'),
q('What does a subsurface scan examine?','Structures beneath the visible surface.','Only the color of the sky.','The public title of a report.','The prefix sub- indicates that the subject is below the surface.'),
q('How does subsurface differ from surface?','Subsurface is underneath; surface is the outermost layer.','Subsurface is above; surface is below.','They both mean the same visible top.','The word directly marks a below-surface location.')
]);
P('subterrane','From Latin subterraneus “underground,” from sub terra “under the earth.” The noun is a cave or room beneath the ground.','Latin subterraneus, from sub terra “under the earth”','The explorers found a dry subterrane beneath the ruined fortress.','探險者在廢棄堡壘下方發現了一處乾燥的地下洞室。','archaeology','Noun: a subterrane is an underground cave or room.','It names a space beneath the ground, not a subterranean animal or a surface tunnel entrance.',M('The subterrane stood on the hilltop in full daylight.','The subterrane lay beneath the hilltop.','A subterrane is a cave or room under the ground, not an exposed hilltop space.','meaning'),[
q('What did the explorers discover when they found a subterrane?','An underground cave or room.','A tower above the fortress.','A ship beneath the ocean.','Subterrane names a cave or room below ground.'),
q('Which clue best supports calling a chamber a subterrane?','It lies beneath the earth.','It is built on an exposed roof.','It floats on a river.','The noun refers to an underground space.'),
q('Which related adjective means “under the ground”?','subterranean','substantial','subsequent','Subterranean is the adjective meaning beneath the ground.'),
q('Why would a cellar not always be called a subterrane?','Subterrane specifically suggests an underground cave or room, not every basement.','A subterrane must be above the ground.','Subterrane means a kind of crop subsidy.','The word is narrower and often refers to an underground room or cave.')
]);
P('subterranean','From Latin subterraneus “underground,” formed from sub terra “under the earth.”','Latin subterraneus, from sub terra “under the earth”','The archaeologists uncovered a subterranean passage beneath the old city.','考古學家在舊城下方發掘出一條地下通道。','archaeology','Adjective: subterranean passage, room, or activity takes place below ground.','It describes something beneath the ground and hidden from the surface.',M('The subterranean tunnel ran visibly across the roof of the tower.','The elevated tunnel ran visibly across the roof of the tower.','A subterranean passage is below ground, unlike a visible route across a roof.','distinction'),[
q('Where is a subterranean passage?','Under the ground.','Above the clouds.','On a building’s roof.','Subterranean means beneath the ground.'),
q('Which sentence uses “subterranean” correctly?','Bats roosted in a subterranean chamber.','The subterranean stars shone above the clouds.','She subterranean the package.','The adjective describes a chamber below the ground.'),
q('What kind of activity is subterranean?','Activity occurring below ground.','Activity performed only at noon.','Activity that happens in outer space.','The adjective can describe something happening under the ground.'),
q('Which word is closest in location to “subterranean”?','underground','overhead','coastal','Subterranean means beneath the earth’s surface.')
]);
P('subtitle','Formed from sub- “below” + title. The word developed both for a secondary title and for written dialogue shown on screen.','sub- “below” + title','A short subtitle beneath the main heading explains the report’s focus.','主標題下方的短副標題說明了報告的重點。','publishing','Noun sense 1: a secondary title; sense 2: written dialogue displayed on a screen.','The sentence uses the secondary-title sense; film subtitles show spoken lines in writing.',M('The book’s subtitle appeared above its main title.','The book’s subtitle appeared below its main title.','A subtitle is a title placed below or after the main title, not above it.','meaning'),[
q('Where does a book subtitle usually appear?','Below or after the main title.','On the back cover only.','Before the author is born.','A subtitle is a secondary title accompanying the main title.'),
q('What does a film subtitle show?','Written words representing spoken lines.','The actors’ salaries.','A second camera angle.','The screen sense is text that viewers read while the dialogue is spoken.'),
q('Which use names a secondary title?','The subtitle “A History of the Harbor” follows the book title.','The subtitle translated every spoken line in the film.','The subtitle ran along the bottom during the movie.','The first example is the title below the main title.'),
q('Which use names screen text?','She turned on subtitles to read the dialogue.','The report’s subtitle clarifies its topic.','The novel has a short subtitle under its title.','Subtitles turned on during a film display spoken dialogue as text.')
]);
P('subtle','From Old French subtil, from Latin subtilis “fine, delicate,” originally “finely woven.” The adjective means hard to notice or cleverly perceptive.','Old French subtil, from Latin subtilis “fine, delicate”','The chef added a subtle hint of lemon that did not overpower the soup.','廚師加入了一絲微妙的檸檬味，沒有蓋過湯的味道。','cooking','Adjective: a subtle flavor/change is slight; a subtle person or argument can be quietly clever.','The example uses the hard-to-notice sense; another sense describes quiet cleverness or fine perception.',M('The subtle change was obvious to every person from across the room.','The striking change was obvious to every person from across the room.','Something subtle is slight and hard to notice, unlike an obvious change.','distinction'),[
q('A subtle flavor is what kind of flavor?','A slight one that is not easy to notice.','An overpowering flavor.','A flavor that is always bitter.','Subtle means slight or difficult to notice.'),
q('Which sentence uses the clever/perceptive sense?','Her subtle analysis caught a quiet contradiction in the speech.','The subtle perfume filled the room strongly.','The soup was subtle because it was frozen.','The first sentence describes careful perception of a fine point.'),
q('What does a subtle change look like?','A small difference that may be easy to miss.','A dramatic transformation visible from miles away.','No difference in any respect.','Subtle describes a slight, hard-to-notice difference.'),
q('Why is “subtle humor” often understated?','Its clever point may be quiet rather than obvious.','It must be shouted loudly.','It cannot contain a joke.','The adjective can mean clever in a quiet or refined way.')
]);
P('subtlety','From Latin subtilitas “fineness, delicacy,” from subtilis “fine, delicate.”','Latin subtilitas, from subtilis “fine, delicate”','The subtlety of the translation lies in a tiny shift in tone.','這個譯文的精妙之處在於語氣上細微的轉變。','literature','Noun: subtlety is delicacy or precision; a subtlety can also be a small distinction.','This context uses the quality sense; plural subtleties often means fine points that are easy to miss.',M('The subtlety of the argument was its loud, obvious repetition of the same claim.','The subtlety of the argument was its quiet distinction between two similar claims.','Subtlety concerns fine, non-obvious distinctions rather than loud repetition.','distinction'),[
q('What does the subtlety of a translation refer to?','Its delicate or precise handling of meaning.','Its number of printed pages.','Its use of only large headlines.','Subtlety is the quality of being delicate, precise, or not obvious.'),
q('A reader misses several subtleties in the poem. What did the reader miss?','Small distinctions that are easy to overlook.','The poem’s entire title page.','Its loudest spoken line only.','A subtlety can be a fine point or small distinction.'),
q('Which word is the adjective related to “subtlety”?','subtle','substance','subside','Subtlety is the noun formed from the adjective subtle.'),
q('Why might a translator need to preserve subtlety?','A small shift in tone can change the intended meaning.','Only the number of letters matters.','Translations must remove all fine distinctions.','Delicate differences in tone and meaning are precisely what subtlety captures.')
]);
P('subtly','Formed from subtle + -ly; subtle comes from Latin subtilis “fine, delicate.”','subtle + -ly; subtle from Latin subtilis “fine, delicate”','The lighting subtly shifted from blue to violet as the scene changed.','場景轉換時，燈光悄然地從藍色變為紫色。','theater','Adverb: subtly modify, change, suggest, or notice a fine difference.','It describes a small or delicate change that may be easy to miss.',M('The lighting subtly changed in a way that was glaringly obvious to everyone.','The lighting changed subtly, so only careful viewers noticed.','A glaringly obvious change is not subtle; subtle means small or hard to notice.','distinction'),[
q('If the lighting changes subtly, how does it change?','By a small, delicate difference.','With a sudden explosion of brightness.','Without changing at all.','Subtly means with a small or hard-to-notice difference.'),
q('Which sentence uses the adverb naturally?','The actor subtly altered her expression.','The actor altered subtly expression her.','The subtle actor expression altered.','The adverb modifies the verb “altered.”'),
q('What does “subtly different” mean?','Different in a small way that may be easy to miss.','Different in every possible way.','Exactly identical.','Subtly marks a delicate difference.'),
q('Why might a designer subtly adjust a shade?','To make a slight color change without drawing strong attention.','To replace the entire design with a blank screen.','To make every object invisible.','A subtle adjustment is small and restrained.')
]);
P('subtract','From Latin subtractere, from sub- “away” + trahere “draw, pull.” It means take one quantity away from another.','Latin subtrahere/subtractere: sub- “away” + trahere “draw”','Subtract the deposit from the total before calculating the balance due.','計算應付餘額前，先從總額中扣除訂金。','mathematics','Verb: subtract A from B; subtract an amount from a total.','The context means remove a number or amount from a larger one; grammar uses “subtract A from B.”',M('Subtract 4 from 10 to get 14.','Subtract 4 from 10 to get 6.','Taking 4 away from 10 leaves 6, not 14.','measurement'),[
q('What is 10 minus 4?','6','14','40','Subtracting 4 from 10 leaves 6.'),
q('Which wording correctly says 3 is taken from 12?','Subtract 3 from 12.','Subtract 12 from 3.','Subtract 3 to 12.','The pattern is subtract the amount removed from the starting total.'),
q('A bill is $80 and a $15 deposit is subtracted. What remains due?','$65','$95','$15','Subtracting the deposit means 80 − 15 = 65.'),
q('Which instruction uses “subtract” outside a math worksheet?','Subtract the returned items from the inventory total.','Subtract the concert after the show.','Subtract the window into the wall.','It means remove an amount or item from a total.')
]);
P('subtraction','From Latin subtractionem, from subtractere “draw away, remove.”','Latin subtractionem, from subtractere “draw away”','The subtraction of shipping costs left the final invoice lower than expected.','扣除運費後，最終發票金額比預期低。','mathematics','Noun: subtraction of A from B; the operation or amount taken away.','It names taking one number or quantity away from another.',M('The subtraction of 5 from 12 produced 17.','The subtraction of 5 from 12 produced 7.','In subtraction, 12 − 5 equals 7, not 17.','measurement'),[
q('Which operation is subtraction?','Taking one quantity away from another.','Combining two quantities into a larger total.','Dividing a quantity into equal parts.','Subtraction removes one number or amount from another.'),
q('What is the result of the subtraction 18 − 6?','12','24','3','Eighteen minus six is twelve.'),
q('Which phrase uses the noun correctly?','the subtraction of fees from the invoice','the subtraction to fees from the invoice','the subtraction for add the fees','“Subtraction of A from B” names the removed amount and starting total.'),
q('How could a shop use subtraction in stock records?','Remove sold units from the starting inventory count.','Add returned units to the number sold only.','Change the color code of every product.','Subtracting sold units gives the remaining stock.')
]);
P('subtractive','Formed from subtract + -ive; subtract derives from Latin subtractere “draw away.”','subtract + -ive; subtract from Latin subtractere “draw away”','The artist used a subtractive process, carving material away from the stone block.','藝術家採用減去式技法，從石塊上逐步雕去材料。','art','Adjective: subtractive processes work by removing material rather than adding it.','The context describes carving away material; in sound or color, subtractive methods likewise remove components.',M('A subtractive sculpture was made by attaching fresh clay to every surface.','An additive sculpture was made by attaching fresh clay to every surface.','Adding material is additive; subtractive work removes material.' ,'distinction'),[
q('How does a subtractive sculptor shape stone?','By removing material.','By attaching new blocks only.','By painting a picture on paper.','Subtractive means working by removal rather than addition.'),
q('Which method is subtractive?','Carving a figure from a block.','Building a figure by stacking clay.','Joining several prefabricated pieces.','Carving removes material from the original block.'),
q('What is the opposite process of subtractive making?','Additive construction.','Subsequent narration.','Subglacial erosion.','Additive methods build by adding; subtractive methods remove.'),
q('Why is a subtractive audio synthesizer named that way?','It shapes a sound by filtering out parts of a richer signal.','It creates sound by adding every instrument live.','It stores audio beneath a surface.','Subtractive synthesis removes frequency components from a source signal.')
]);
P('subtropic','Formed from sub- “near/below” + tropic, ultimately from Greek tropos “turn.”','sub- “near” + tropic, from Greek tropos “turn”','The subtropic island has mild winters and hot, humid summers.','這座副熱帶島嶼冬季溫和，夏季炎熱潮濕。','climate','Adjective: subtropic relates to regions just outside the tropics.','It describes a region bordering the tropical belt, not a place inside the hottest tropical zone.',M('The subtropic station sits directly on the equator in the deep tropics.','The tropical station sits directly on the equator in the deep tropics.','Subtropic refers to regions just outside the tropics; the equator is in the tropics.','distinction'),[
q('Where is a subtropic region located?','Just outside the tropics.','At the North Pole.','Exactly at the center of a glacier.','The prefix marks a region near but outside the tropical belt.'),
q('Which climate note could describe a subtropic coast?','It borders the tropics but lies outside the tropical belt.','It remains frozen all year at the pole.','It lies beneath a permanent ice sheet.','Subtropic means relating to regions just beyond the tropics.'),
q('Choose the correct adjective: “The ___ belt borders the tropics.”','subtropic','substantial','submerged','Subtropic is the climate-region adjective.'),
q('How does subtropic differ from tropical?','Subtropic is just outside the tropical zone.','Subtropic is always colder than the Arctic.','They name unrelated geological layers.','The word’s prefix sub- places it near or below the tropical region.')
]);
P('subtropical','Formed from sub- “near/below” + tropical; tropical ultimately derives from Greek tropikos, related to tropos “turn.”','sub- “near” + tropical; tropical from Greek tropikos','Many subtropical gardens can grow citrus outdoors through mild winters.','許多亞熱帶花園在溫和冬季也能於戶外種植柑橘。','climate','Adjective: subtropical climate or region borders the hot tropical belt.','It describes climates and places bordering the tropics, often with warm conditions but some seasonal variation.',M('The subtropical garden is located at the geographic North Pole.','The arctic garden is located at the geographic North Pole.','Subtropical climates border the tropics and do not describe the polar region.','distinction'),[
q('Which location is most likely subtropical?','A warm region just beyond the tropics.','A polar ice cap.','A cave far beneath a glacier.','Subtropical places border the tropical belt.'),
q('What could grow in a subtropical garden?','Citrus trees in a region with mild winters.','Only lichens under polar ice.','No plants because the climate is always frozen.','The adjective suggests warm regions outside the hottest tropics.'),
q('Which phrase is natural?','subtropical climate','subtropical the climate','climate subtropically','The adjective modifies the noun climate.'),
q('Why are subtropical and tropical not perfectly interchangeable?','Subtropical regions border the tropical belt rather than lying fully within it.','Subtropical refers to underground rooms.','Tropical always means below freezing.','The prefix sub- marks proximity to, but outside, the tropics.')
]);
P('suburb','From Latin suburbium, from sub- “near/below” + urbs “city.” It names a residential area outside a city center.','Latin suburbium: sub- “near” + urbs “city”','They moved to a quiet suburb with a direct train into the city center.','他們搬到一個安靜的郊區，搭火車可直達市中心。','urban life','Noun: a suburb is an area outside a city’s central part; plural suburbs.','The sentence uses the common residential district sense; another source sense stresses a mainly residential edge district.',M('She lives in a suburb at the center of the downtown business district.','She lives in a suburb on the edge of the city.','A suburb lies outside the central part of a city, not in its downtown core.','meaning'),[
q('Where is a suburb generally located?','Outside the central part of a city.','At the exact center of a city hall.','Only in the countryside with no town nearby.','A suburb is an area where people live beyond the city center.'),
q('What does “the suburbs” often refer to?','Residential districts around a city.','The city’s central business towers only.','A mountain range far from any settlement.','Plural suburbs commonly refers to residential areas on the edge of a town or city.'),
q('Which sentence uses “suburb” as a countable noun?','They rented a house in a suburb.','They suburb the house each summer.','The suburbly street was quiet.','A suburb is a place that can be counted: a suburb.'),
q('How does a suburb differ from downtown?','A suburb is outside the central area; downtown is the city center.','A suburb is always beneath downtown.','Downtown is a person who lives outside a city.','The defining contrast is location relative to the urban center.')
]);
P('suburban','From Latin suburbanus, from suburbium “area near a city,” itself from sub- + urbs “city.”','Latin suburbanus, from suburbium “area near a city”','A suburban street lined with small gardens connects the homes to the train station.','一條種滿小花園的郊區街道連接住宅與火車站。','urban life','Adjective: suburban homes, life, or streets belong to residential areas around a city.','It describes something belonging to the residential area around a city.',M('Their suburban apartment stood in the middle of the city’s central business district.','Their downtown apartment stood in the middle of the city’s central business district.','Suburban refers to the area around a city, not its central business district.','distinction'),[
q('What is a suburban neighborhood?','A residential area around a city.','The center of a national park.','A room beneath a building.','Suburban means belonging to the residential area around a city.'),
q('Which noun can “suburban” naturally modify?','commute','yesterday','quickly','Suburban is an adjective used with nouns such as commute, home, or street.'),
q('A suburban commuter travels from where?','A residential district around the city.','The city’s deepest subway tunnel only.','A farm with no relation to a city.','The adjective identifies the commuter’s home area outside the center.'),
q('Why is “suburban center” not always the same as “downtown”?','Suburban describes an area around the city, while downtown is its central district.','Suburban means underground.','Downtown means a person’s house.','The terms locate places differently in relation to the city center.')
]);
P('suburbanite','Formed from suburban + -ite, a suffix naming a person associated with a place or group.','suburban + -ite, person associated with a place','The suburbanite takes the early train into the city for work.','這名郊區居民搭乘早班火車進城上班。','urban life','Noun: a suburbanite is a person who lives in a suburb.','It identifies a resident by home location, not a visitor who merely travels there.',M('The suburbanite lives downtown and has never lived outside the city center.','The suburbanite lives in a residential district outside the city center.','A suburbanite is someone whose home is in a suburb, not downtown.','meaning'),[
q('Who is a suburbanite?','A person who lives in a suburb.','A person who works only in a subway tunnel.','A resident of a polar research station.','The noun means someone whose home is in a suburb.'),
q('What clue identifies a character as a suburbanite?','Their home is in a residential district outside the city center.','They visit a suburb once on vacation.','They read a book about urban planning.','Residence, not a brief visit, defines the term.'),
q('Which word is the base for “suburbanite”?','suburban','substance','submarine','Suburbanite is formed from suburban plus the person suffix -ite.'),
q('Why is a tourist not automatically a suburbanite?','The word describes where someone lives, not just where they visit.','A tourist must live in the city center.','The word refers only to a train conductor.','A suburbanite is defined by having a home in a suburb.')
]);
P('suburbanization','Formed from suburban + -ization, naming the process of making or moving into suburban areas.','suburban + -ization; suburban from Latin suburbanus “near a city”','Rapid suburbanization replaced farmland with housing and shopping centers around the city.','快速近郊化使城市周邊的農地被住宅與購物中心取代。','urban planning','Noun: suburbanization is the movement of housing and business from a city into surrounding suburbs.','It names a process of outward growth, not the ordinary act of moving within the city center.',M('Suburbanization brought new housing into the city center while leaving the surrounding suburbs empty.','Urban renewal brought new housing into the city center while suburbanization expanded the surrounding suburbs.','Suburbanization describes growth into surrounding suburbs, not inward development of the central city.','distinction'),[
q('What does suburbanization describe?','Housing and businesses spreading from a city into its surrounding suburbs.','A city’s population moving only into its downtown core.','The process of building rooms beneath the ground.','Suburbanization is outward movement into suburban areas.'),
q('Which change is an example of suburbanization?','A new residential district spreads beyond the city edge.','A downtown office tower is renovated in place.','A farm is converted into a glacier.','Growth of housing beyond the city into surrounding suburbs matches the term.'),
q('What may rapid suburbanization do to land near a city?','Replace farmland with homes and businesses.','Turn all nearby land into open ocean.','Move the city’s central station underground.','Outward growth often changes surrounding land use.'),
q('Why is suburbanization different from urbanization?','It specifically concerns growth into suburbs around a city.','It means a tax paid to city residents.','It describes any type of weather change.','The “suburban-” element identifies the surrounding residential areas.')
]);
P('suburbs','Plural of suburb, from Latin suburbium “area near a city,” from sub- + urbs “city.”','plural of suburb; Latin suburbium “area near a city”','The suburbs emptied during the day as many residents commuted downtown.','白天，許多居民通勤到市中心，郊區因而變得冷清。','urban life','Plural noun: the suburbs are residential areas around a city, often with commuters.','It refers to districts on a city’s edge, not the central business area.',M('The suburbs are the city’s central financial district beside the main government offices.','The downtown district is the city’s central financial area; the suburbs lie around it.','Suburbs are around the city center, not the central business district itself.','meaning'),[
q('What do “the suburbs” usually mean?','Residential areas around a city.','The city’s central financial district only.','The countryside with no nearby town.','The plural commonly names the areas around a city where people live.'),
q('Why might the suburbs be quieter during working hours?','Many residents commute to jobs in the city center.','All residents work from the same suburban park.','The suburbs are permanently underwater.','The source sense notes that residents travel to work in the center.'),
q('Which phrase is natural?','a house in the suburbs','a house at suburbs','a house suburbly','The standard expression is “in the suburbs.”'),
q('What is the contrast between suburbs and downtown?','Suburbs surround the central area; downtown is the city center.','Suburbs are underground; downtown is above the clouds.','They are two names for the same single street.','The terms distinguish residential outskirts from the central district.')
]);
P('subversive','From subvert + -ive; subvert comes from Latin subvertere “overturn, overthrow,” from sub- + vertere “turn.”','subvert + -ive; Latin subvertere “overturn”','The regime censored subversive pamphlets that challenged its authority.','政權審查了挑戰其權威的顛覆性小冊子。','politics','Adjective: subversive writing or activity seeks to weaken authority or accepted ideas from within.','It describes an undermining purpose, often concealed; it does not simply mean unconventional or unpopular.',M('The subversive pamphlet openly strengthened the ruler’s authority and discouraged all criticism.','The loyalist pamphlet strengthened the ruler’s authority and discouraged all criticism.','Subversive material seeks to weaken authority or accepted ideas, not reinforce them.','distinction'),[
q('What is the purpose of subversive writing under a strict regime?','To weaken authority or accepted ideas.','To praise every policy without question.','To explain how to grow a garden.','Subversive means seeking to weaken authority or accepted ideas.'),
q('Which act is most clearly subversive?','Secretly organizing to undermine an authoritarian system.','Publishing the official schedule of public holidays.','Repairing a broken classroom chair.','The secret effort to weaken a system fits the adjective.'),
q('Does “subversive” simply mean unusual?','No; it suggests an effort to undermine authority or accepted ideas.','Yes; it means any rare color.','No; it means physically below ground only.','The meaning concerns weakening a system, not merely being different.'),
q('Which noun naturally follows “subversive” in a political report?','activity','temperature','subtraction','Subversive activity is a standard collocation for actions that undermine authority.')
]);
P('subvert','From Latin subvertere “overturn, overthrow,” from sub- “from below” + vertere “turn.”','Latin subvertere “overturn, overthrow”','The whistleblower exposed a plan to subvert the election through forged ballots.','吹哨者揭露了一項企圖透過偽造選票顛覆選舉的計畫。','politics','Verb: subvert a system or institution, often secretly or by weakening it from within.','It means damage or overthrow an established system; it need not mean a sudden open attack.',M('The group subverted the voting system by protecting every ballot and preventing fraud.','The group protected the voting system by safeguarding every ballot and preventing fraud.','Safeguarding ballots protects a system rather than damaging or overthrowing it.','meaning'),[
q('What does it mean to subvert an institution?','To damage or overthrow it, often secretly.','To formally congratulate its members.','To measure its building.','Subvert means undermine or overthrow an established system.'),
q('Which action could subvert an election?','Secretly forging ballots to distort the result.','Counting ballots under transparent rules.','Publishing the polling-place locations.','Fraudulent ballot forgery undermines the election system.'),
q('What is the base verb in “subversion”?','subvert','suburban','subside','Subversion is the noun related to the verb subvert.'),
q('Why can a subversive plan be hard to detect?','It may seek to weaken a system secretly from within.','It is always announced in a parade.','It means to arrange a public picnic.','The term often describes secret efforts to undermine a system.')
]);
P('subway','Formed from sub- “under” + way. In American English it commonly means an underground city railway; it can also mean a pedestrian passage under a busy road.','sub- “under” + way','We took the subway across town during the rush hour.','尖峰時段我們搭地鐵穿越了整座城。','transportation','Noun sense 1: an underground electric city railway; sense 2: an under-road pedestrian passage.','This sentence uses the railway sense; the same word can name a walkway passing below a busy road.',M('We took the subway to cross the city, but it was a pedestrian tunnel with no trains.','We took the subway train to cross the city.','For city rail travel, subway names the underground railway; a pedestrian tunnel is its other source sense.','meaning'),[
q('What does “take the subway to work” usually mean in a city?','Ride the underground electric railway.','Walk through a passage under a road.','Travel by airplane above the city.','The common American noun sense is an underground urban railway.'),
q('A sign directs walkers through a subway under a busy road. What is it?','An underground pedestrian passage.','A train station above the clouds.','A suburban business office.','Subway can also mean a path under a busy road.'),
q('Which sentence uses subway as a railway?','The subway train arrived at platform three.','We crossed the highway through the subway.','The subway passage was closed to walkers.','The train and platform show the railway sense.'),
q('Which feature distinguishes a subway passage from a subway train system?','The passage lets pedestrians walk under a road; the system carries riders by rail.','The passage flies between cities.','The train is a room below a house.','The entry has two senses: transit railway and pedestrian underpass.')
]);
P('succeed','From Latin succedere “come after, go near,” from sub- “under, after” + cedere “go.” English uses include achieving a goal and following someone in a position.','Latin succedere: sub- “after” + cedere “go”','After months of careful testing, the engineers succeeded in stabilizing the bridge.','經過數月仔細測試，工程師成功讓橋梁恢復穩定。','engineering','Verb sense 1: succeed in doing something; sense 2: succeed someone in a role or come next in sequence.','The example means achieve the intended result; “succeed to” or “succeed someone” can mean take over a position.',M('After months of testing, the engineers succeeded to stabilizing the bridge.','After months of testing, the engineers succeeded in stabilizing the bridge.','For achieving an action, the usual pattern is succeed in + -ing, not succeed to + -ing.','collocation'),[
q('If the engineers succeeded in the repair, what happened?','They achieved the result they were trying for.','They gave up before starting.','They followed the engineers in a queue.','Succeed in doing something means achieve the attempted result.'),
q('In “Mira succeeded her mother as director,” what does succeeded mean?','She came after her mother in the position.','She helped her mother finish a task.','She failed to become director.','This sense means take over a position after someone.'),
q('Which construction is correct for achieving a goal?','succeed in solving the problem','succeed to solving the problem','succeed at to solve the problem','The usual form is succeed in + gerund.'),
q('Which sentence uses the “come after in time” sense?','A new model succeeded the original next year.','The team succeeded in its trial.','She succeeded to her grandfather’s estate.','The model came after the original in sequence.')
]);
P('succeed to','From succeed, ultimately Latin succedere “come after”; the phrase “succeed to” means take over a title, office, or property.','succeed + to; from Latin succedere “come after”','When the monarch abdicated, her eldest child was expected to succeed to the throne.','君主退位後，長子預計將繼承王位。','government','Verb phrase: succeed to a title, position, or property.','It means take over from someone else; it is distinct from succeed in doing something successfully.',M('The heir succeeded to the throne by refusing to take the title or office.','The heir declined to succeed to the throne and take the title.','Succeed to a throne means take over the title; refusing it is declining succession.','meaning'),[
q('What does an heir do when she succeeds to the throne?','Takes over the royal title or position.','Wins a race against the monarch.','Submits a report to the court.','Succeed to means take over a title or position from someone.'),
q('Which object naturally follows “succeed to”?','the estate','the homework in','the problem at','The phrase takes a title, position, or property as its object.'),
q('How is “succeed to office” different from “succeed in office”?','The first means take over the position; the second means perform well while holding it.','Both mean fail to get elected.','The first means achieve a task; the second means inherit property.','Succeed to is succession into the role; succeed in describes achievement within it.'),
q('Which sentence uses the phrase correctly?','She succeeded to the family business after her uncle retired.','She succeeded to finish the report by noon.','She succeeded the family business with a report.','Succeed to is used for taking over property or a position.')
]);
P('succeeding','Present participle of succeed; ultimately from Latin succedere “come after.” As an adjective it means following in sequence.','present participle of succeed; Latin succedere “come after”','In the succeeding chapter, the narrator explains why the expedition turned back.','在接下來的章節中，敘述者說明了探險隊折返的原因。','publishing','Adjective: succeeding day/chapter/generation follows another in sequence.','It points to what comes next, rather than what came before.',M('The succeeding chapter appeared before the opening chapter.','The preceding chapter appeared before the opening chapter.','Succeeding means following; a chapter before the opening is not succeeding it.','distinction'),[
q('What is the succeeding chapter?','The chapter that follows the current one.','The chapter that came before it.','The chapter that was never written.','Succeeding means following in sequence.'),
q('Which item is succeeding in a timeline?','The event occurring after the one just mentioned.','The event that happened a year earlier.','An event outside the timeline.','The adjective places the event next in sequence.'),
q('Which phrase is natural?','the succeeding generation','the succeeding before generation','the generation succeedingly','Succeeding functions as an adjective modifying generation.'),
q('How does “succeeding day” relate to “following day”?','They both mean the day that comes next.','They mean the day before.','They refer only to the same hour.','Succeeding describes what follows in time.')
]);
P('success','From Latin successus, past participle of succedere “come after, advance.” It refers to achieving a goal and can also name a person or thing that does very well.','Latin successus, from succedere “advance, come after”','The clean-water campaign was a success: every village gained a working well.','潔淨飲水運動非常成功：每個村莊都獲得了一口可用的水井。','public health','Noun sense 1: achievement of an intended result; sense 2: a person or thing that does very well.','The example uses the outcome sense; success can also be a successful person or popular thing.',M('The campaign was a success because none of its villages received clean water.','The campaign failed because none of its villages received clean water.','Success means achieving the desired result; no village receiving water means the campaign failed.','meaning'),[
q('Why was the clean-water campaign a success?','It achieved the goal of giving every village a working well.','It canceled every water project.','It left all villages without water.','Success is achieving what was wanted.'),
q('In “The new bakery is a success,” what may success mean?','A business or thing that does very well.','A legal duty to pay a subsidy.','A grammatical conjunction.','Success can name a person or thing that performs very well.'),
q('Which phrase uses success as the result of an effort?','Her recovery was a success.','She succeeded to the title.','A successful person arrived.','Recovery being a success refers to an achieved outcome.'),
q('What is the likely opposite of the campaign’s success?','Failure to achieve its goal.','A successor taking office.','A subsequent meeting.','Success means achieving the goal; failure means not achieving it.')
]);
P('successful','Formed from success + -ful; success comes from Latin successus “advance, outcome.”','success + -ful; success from Latin successus','The successful rescue team brought all stranded hikers down safely.','成功的救援隊安全地將所有受困健行者帶下山。','emergency response','Adjective: successful attempt/result/person; can also mean prosperous in work or life.','The context means achieving the desired result; another sense can mean having money, fame, or a good career.',M('The rescue was successful because the hikers remained stranded and were never found.','The rescue was unsuccessful because the hikers remained stranded and were never found.','A rescue that fails to find the hikers is not successful.' ,'meaning'),[
q('A successful rescue had what result?','The hikers were brought to safety.','The rescuers left before searching.','The hikers were never located.','Successful means achieving the result that was wanted.'),
q('A successful architect in the career sense may be what?','Established and prosperous through work.','Unable to complete any project.','Hidden beneath a glacier.','The source includes an accomplished or prosperous person sense.'),
q('Which noun is correctly modified?','a successful experiment','a successfulness experiment','an experiment successful','Successful is the adjective used before a noun.'),
q('What does “a successful attempt” mean?','An attempt that achieved its intended result.','An attempt that was never made.','An attempt that failed at every stage.','The adjective marks achievement of the desired outcome.')
]);
P('successfully','Formed from successful + -ly; successful is success + -ful, from Latin successus “outcome.”','successful + -ly; success from Latin successus','The team successfully restored power to the clinic before nightfall.','團隊在天黑前成功恢復了診所的供電。','engineering','Adverb: successfully complete, restore, pass, or carry out something.','It describes an action that produces the intended result or is done without error.',M('The team successfully restored power, but the clinic remained without electricity.','The team unsuccessfully restored power, so the clinic remained without electricity.','If the clinic still has no power, the restoration did not succeed.','meaning'),[
q('What does “the team successfully restored power” mean?','The restoration achieved its intended result.','The team tried but left the clinic without electricity.','The power was never interrupted.','Successfully means in a way that produces the intended result.'),
q('Which sentence uses the adverb properly?','She successfully completed the certification exam.','She completed successfully the her exam.','She successful completed the exam.','Successfully modifies the verb completed.'),
q('If a test was passed successfully, what is true?','The candidate passed it without failing.','The candidate did not take it.','The candidate failed every section.','The adverb describes a result achieved without making a mistake.'),
q('Why is “successfully” more exact than “carefully” in a result report?','It says the desired outcome was achieved.','It says the person moved slowly.','It says the task was expensive.','Carefully describes manner; successfully describes achieving the result.')
]);
P('succession','From Latin successionem, from succedere “come after.” It can mean a series in sequence or the act/order of taking over a title or job.','Latin successionem, from succedere “come after”','A succession of storms delayed the harbor repairs for nearly a week.','一連串暴風雨使港口修復工程延誤了將近一週。','weather','Noun sense 1: a succession is a series following one after another; sense 2: succession to a role is taking it over.','The context uses a series of events; the term also describes taking over a job, title, or property.',M('A succession of storms means one storm occurred, followed by years with no further events.','A succession of storms means several storms occurred one after another.','A succession is a series of similar events following each other.' ,'meaning'),[
q('What is a succession of storms?','Several storms following one another.','One storm followed by no other event.','A single quiet afternoon.','A succession can be a number of similar things occurring one after another.'),
q('What does “the succession to the throne” concern?','Who takes over the royal position and in what order.','How to predict tomorrow’s weather.','Which crops grow in a suburb.','Succession also means taking over a title or the order of successors.'),
q('Which phrase uses the series sense?','a succession of small earthquakes','a successor to the company chair','to succeed in a task','A succession of earthquakes is a series of events.'),
q('Why is a succession of events different from a succession to office?','One is a series; the other is transfer into a position.','Both only mean a repeated storm.','One is a type of underground path.','The noun has both a sequence sense and a transfer-of-position sense.')
]);
P('successional','Formed from succession + -al; succession comes from Latin successio, from succedere “follow.” In ecology it describes gradual replacement of one community by another.','succession + -al; succession from Latin successio “following”','Successional change after the wildfire gradually brought shrubs and young trees back to the hillside.','野火後的演替變化逐漸讓灌木與幼樹重新覆蓋山坡。','ecology','Adjective: successional change or stage concerns ecological succession.','The adjective is field-specific: it describes gradual replacement of one biological community by another.',M('Successional change after the fire meant the ecosystem stayed permanently unchanged.','Successional change after the fire meant the ecosystem gradually shifted to a new community.','Ecological succession is gradual community replacement, not permanent lack of change.','meaning'),[
q('What does successional change in ecology involve?','Gradual replacement of one community of organisms by another.','A single organism changing its name.','A city’s subway extending under a road.','Successional describes gradual ecological community replacement.'),
q('Which phrase is most likely ecological?','successional plant communities','successional train ticket','successional subtraction table','Plant communities can change through ecological succession.'),
q('After a wildfire, why might new shrubs represent a successional stage?','They form part of the gradual recovery and replacement of communities.','They prove that no ecosystem change occurred.','They are a taxonomic rank under an order.','Successional stages are part of changing ecological communities.'),
q('Which noun does “successional” most directly modify in ecology?','change','throne','magazine','Successional change is a standard ecological phrase.')
]);
P('successive','From Latin successivus, from successio “following.”','Latin successivus, from successio “following”','The runner won three successive races without a loss.','這名跑者連續贏得三場比賽，未嘗敗績。','sports','Adjective: successive days, wins, or events happen one after another without interruption.','It emphasizes an unbroken series in sequence.',M('The runner won three successive races, with a month-long defeat between each win.','The runner won three separate races, with a defeat between each win.','Successive events follow one another without interruption; intervening defeats break the series.','distinction'),[
q('Three successive wins occur how?','One after another without an interruption.','At the same instant.','With a loss between every win.','Successive describes an uninterrupted series.'),
q('Which example is successive?','Rain fell on five consecutive days.','Rain fell in January and again in July only.','Rain stopped forever after one shower.','Five consecutive days form an unbroken sequence.'),
q('Which noun naturally follows “successive”?','attempts','substance','suburb','Successive attempts are attempts occurring one after another.'),
q('How does successive differ from “occasional”?','Successive events follow one another in a series; occasional events are scattered.','They both mean uninterrupted.','Successive means underground.','The adjective signals sequence without interruption.')
]);
P('successively','Formed from successive + -ly; successive comes from Latin successio “following.”','successive + -ly; from Latin successio “following”','The three speakers presented their findings successively, one after another.','三位講者依序一位接一位地報告研究結果。','communication','Adverb: successively means in sequence, with each item following the previous one.','It describes order in a series, not necessarily speed or simultaneous action.',M('The three speakers presented successively at the same time.','The three speakers presented simultaneously at the same time.','Successively means one after another; simultaneous presentation occurs at the same time.','distinction'),[
q('If applicants are interviewed successively, how are they interviewed?','One after another.','All at exactly the same time.','Only after being rejected.','Successively means in succession, each following the previous one.'),
q('Which sentence uses “successively” correctly?','The lights flashed successively from left to right.','The lights successively flashed all at one instant.','She successively the lights.','The first sentence describes an ordered sequence.'),
q('What does the adverb emphasize in “the team won successively”?','The wins came in sequence.','The wins happened in different centuries.','The team was replaced by a successor.','Successively marks one following another.'),
q('Which word contrasts with “successively” in an event schedule?','simultaneously','subsequently','repeatedly','Simultaneously means at the same time, unlike one-after-another succession.')
]);
P('successor','From Latin successor, from succedere “follow after.”','Latin successor, from succedere “follow after”','The retiring editor trained her successor before leaving the magazine.','即將退休的編輯在離開雜誌前培訓了接任者。','publishing','Noun: a successor takes over a job, title, or position from another person.','The context names the person who will take over the editor’s role.',M('The successor left the job before the previous editor had ever held it.','The predecessor left the job before the successor took over.','A successor follows someone into the role; the original reverses the order of succession.','meaning'),[
q('Who is the retiring editor’s successor?','The person who takes over the editor’s job.','The person who held the job before her.','The magazine’s chief reader.','A successor takes over a job or position from another.'),
q('Which phrase is standard?','successor to the director','successor than the director','successor under to director','The usual construction is successor to a person or role.'),
q('What is a successor to an old phone model?','A later model that replaces it.','A model that was made before it.','A phone battery’s chemical substrate.','A successor comes after or takes the place of the earlier model.'),
q('How does a successor differ from a predecessor?','The successor comes after; the predecessor came before.','They both mean the same future replacement.','A predecessor is always a family member.','The prefix-related temporal roles point in opposite directions.')
]);
P('succinct','From Latin succinctus, past participle of succingere “gird, tuck up.” English developed the sense “compact, concise.”','Latin succinctus, from succingere “gird, tuck up”','Her succinct summary captures the decision in three sentences.','她簡潔的摘要用三個句子概括了決定。','writing','Adjective: a succinct explanation uses few words without omitting what matters.','It praises concise completeness, not merely shortness that leaves out essential information.',M('His succinct report omitted the decision, evidence, and conclusion.','His short report omitted the decision, evidence, and conclusion.','A report that leaves out what matters is short but not succinct in the positive sense.','distinction'),[
q('What makes a summary succinct?','It uses few words while preserving the important point.','It is long and repeats every detail.','It contains no conclusion.','Succinct means brief without leaving out what matters.'),
q('Which is the best example of a succinct instruction?','“Turn left at the bridge.”','A three-page history of the bridge before giving directions.','“Go.” with no location or destination.','The first gives the needed direction concisely; the others are overlong or incomplete.'),
q('Which noun is naturally modified by “succinct”?','answer','mountainousness','subsidy','A succinct answer is a concise one.'),
q('Why is “succinct” more positive than “brief” in some contexts?','It suggests concise wording that still includes what matters.','It always means confusingly short.','It means the text is written underground.','Succinct includes the idea of retaining the essential point.')
]);
P('succinctly','Formed from succinct + -ly; succinct comes from Latin succinctus “girded, tucked up.”','succinct + -ly; from Latin succinctus','The chair succinctly stated the motion before calling for a vote.','主席簡明扼要地陳述了動議，接著請大家表決。','meetings','Adverb: succinctly explain, state, or summarize a point in few words without losing it.','It describes concise expression that retains the essential point.',M('She explained the issue succinctly in forty pages of repetition.','She explained the issue at length in forty pages of repetition.','Forty pages of repeated detail are not concise; succinctly means briefly but completely.','distinction'),[
q('If a chair states a motion succinctly, what does the chair do?','Gives the essential point in few words.','Repeats every detail for an hour.','Leaves out the motion entirely.','Succinctly means concisely without losing the point.'),
q('Which sentence uses the adverb correctly?','He succinctly summarized the three findings.','He succinctly summary the findings.','He summarized succinct the findings.','Succinctly is an adverb modifying “summarized.”'),
q('Which is a succinct response to “When is the meeting?”','At noon.','The meeting, whose history began many years ago, will occur at the time when the clock reaches...','Maybe sometime next month, but I do not know.','“At noon” gives the complete answer in very few words.'),
q('Why should a succinct explanation still include the key reason?','Succinct wording is brief but does not leave out what matters.','Succinct means only one word regardless of meaning.','Reasons are never part of explanations.','The adverb preserves the point while using few words.')
]);
P('succulent','From Latin succulentus “full of juice,” from succus “juice.” As a noun it also names a plant with fleshy water-storing parts.','Latin succulentus “full of juice,” from succus “juice”','The grilled peaches stayed succulent inside despite their charred edges.','烤桃子外層雖然焦香，內部仍然多汁。','cooking','Adjective: succulent fruit or meat is juicy; noun: a succulent stores water in fleshy parts.','This context uses the juicy-food sense; the countable noun refers to a water-storing plant.',M('The succulent peach was dry and had no juice at all.','The dry peach was no longer succulent.','Succulent food is juicy; completely dry food does not fit that meaning.','distinction'),[
q('What does a succulent peach taste like?','Juicy and moist.','Completely dry and dusty.','Extremely salty with no fruit flavor.','Succulent means full of juice, especially of fruit or meat.'),
q('What is a succulent in a plant shop?','A plant with fleshy parts that store water.','A plant that can live only underwater.','A mushroom with no leaves.','The noun sense names a water-storing plant.'),
q('Which sentence uses “succulent” as a noun?','She placed a small succulent by the sunny window.','The succulent mango dripped juice.','The meat was succulent after roasting.','The first sentence names a plant; the other examples use the adjective.'),
q('Why might roasted meat be described as succulent?','It remains juicy rather than dry.','It has no liquid at all.','It has been placed under the ground.','The adjective refers to food full of juice.')
]);
P('succumb','From Latin succumbere “lie down under, yield,” from sub- “under” + cumbere “lie.” It means yield to pressure or desire, or die from disease/be destroyed.','Latin succumbere “lie under, yield”','The exhausted climbers refused to succumb to the cold before reaching the shelter.','筋疲力盡的登山者拒絕在抵達避難所前向寒冷屈服。','outdoors','Verb sense 1: succumb to pressure, temptation, or disease; sense 2: die from an illness or be destroyed by a force.','The context uses yielding to a force; in medical reporting, succumb to an illness can mean die from it.',M('The patient succumbed to the infection and made a complete recovery without treatment.','The patient overcame the infection and made a complete recovery without treatment.','To succumb to a disease can mean die from it or be overcome by it, not recover successfully.','meaning'),[
q('What does it mean to succumb to pressure?','To give in to it.','To increase pressure on others.','To measure it with a thermometer.','Succumb means stop resisting and yield.'),
q('In a medical report, “she succumbed to the illness” may mean what?','She died from the illness.','She recovered instantly from it.','She gave the illness to a doctor.','Succumb to a disease can mean die from it.'),
q('Which preposition completes the verb? “He succumbed ___ temptation.”','to','at','with','The usual construction is succumb to a force, desire, or disease.'),
q('Which action shows someone did not succumb to a craving?','They resisted it and chose not to act on it.','They gave in and ate the forbidden food.','They died from an infection.','Resisting means not yielding; succumbing means giving in.')
]);
})();
